  ('11ae20fe-cada-5b7b-a195-09bdff1c0989'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '11ae20fe-cada-5b7b-a195-09bdff1c0989'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'How World Works', E'Throughout the 18th century, mathematicians, scientists and philosophers researched, discussed, and published their investigations into how the world worked, while engineers and inventors developed new and successful machines and processes. The {{1}} theories inspired greater invention, and more technology encouraged theoretical scientists to make further discoveries in medicine, biology, mechanics, physics, and chemistry. By 1800, the new machines {{2}} brought revolutionary changes to the workplace, transportation and communications, and eventually to the home. Some of these inventions simply made it easier to produce things on a large scale such as textile machines and foundries, {{3}} produced large quantities of cloth and metal objects quickly and cheaply. But some inventions {{4}} completely new possibilities such as the first batteries, steamboats, and locomotives. It would take decades for some of these inventions {{5}} a big impact on the world. {{6}} their creation, and the sheer amount of imagination and risk-taking {{7}} , marked the beginning of a modern, global, technologically based economy of the kind that we live in today.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.latest, 2.had, 3.which, 4.brought, 5.to make, 6.Yet, 7.involved', NULL, NULL),
  ('11aed1ab-eda1-5585-b07f-92bc4859ad28'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '11aed1ab-eda1-5585-b07f-92bc4859ad28'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Road Taken', E'The poem “The Road Not Taken” by Robert Frost is about the {{1}} that one makes in life. It tells about a man who comes to a {{2}} in the road he is traveling upon. He feels {{3}} that he cannot travel {{4}} paths as he must choose one. Frost uses this divergence in the road to represent a point in the man’s life where he has to choose the {{5}} he wishes to take in life.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.choices, 2.fork, 3.sorry, 4.both, 5.direction', NULL, NULL),
