  ('16e16e4a-1c0a-51bb-b1b2-9ddb49c5e247'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'HIGHLIGHT_CORRECT_SUMMARY', E'HIGHLIGHT_CORRECT_SUMMARY', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '16e16e4a-1c0a-51bb-b1b2-9ddb49c5e247'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'HCS', E'As you know there has been an unresolved debate as to when and why early humans first started to use fire. However, recent new findings from a study in South Africa have been the talk of the anthropological community. So what are these findings? Well, prehistoric ash and the remains of burnt bones are evidence that early humans used fire much longer ago than previously envisaged. 300,000 years earlier than believed, in fact. Scientists have found evidence of multiple fires, ash and bones deep inside a cave which means it is highly improbable the fires were started naturally. One question that still remains unanswered is why they were using fire. Some researchers are citing the burnt bones as proof that our ancestors cooked the meat, but it has also been suggested they ate the meat raw and then tossed the bones into the fire.\n\nClick on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', '2a029fa7-3f9f-5d7f-ac70-ac9fda0d1c22'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Scientists believe they are now nearer to finding an answer to the question of when humans first started to use fire, and it is much earlier than had been thought. Nevertheless, there is still some disagreement among researchers around what early humans actually used the fire for.', NULL, NULL),
  ('16e3482d-2738-519e-8a6d-e6f678c15d3c'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '16e3482d-2738-519e-8a6d-e6f678c15d3c'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'ASQ', E'What do we call the remains of a meteoroid that reaches the Earth''s surface?', '48cb5ae0-9f89-5a5d-a231-1c8c7eb898cd'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Meteorite', NULL, NULL),
  ('16e43b79-8f62-5524-a1a0-612ab2b4ccad'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '16e43b79-8f62-5524-a1a0-612ab2b4ccad'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Librarians', E'The evolving role of librarians in the digital era highlights a significant shift from traditional book curation to becoming guardians of the digital information age. Today''s librarians are required to possess a multifaceted skill {{1}} , navigating the complexities of digital databases, e-books, and online research platforms with the same ease as the Dewey Decimal System. They play a pivotal role in educating the public on digital literacy and ensure that communities have access to, and understand how to use, the vast resources {{2}} in the digital domain. This includes managing digital archives, {{3}} online collections, and providing guidance on evaluating the credibility of online information. {{4}} , librarians are at the forefront of advocating for digital privacy and freedom of information, ensuring that the digital transition enhances, rather than {{5}} , public access to knowledge. As libraries transform into tech hubs, the librarian''s role as an information specialist is more crucial than ever, steering communities towards a future where information is accessible to all.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.set, 2.available, 3.curating, 4.Furthermore, 5.impedes', NULL, NULL),
  ('16e43bb4-7d32-5de1-99f0-2d22408df649'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '16e43bb4-7d32-5de1-99f0-2d22408df649'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'RS', NULL, 'aa658b76-0c90-5258-8310-32c30fb256c2'::uuid, NULL, E'Despite my friends'' desire to study abroad, I prefer home.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
