  ('1dc93ede-edef-5740-8aa8-e7710d436d80'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '1dc93ede-edef-5740-8aa8-e7710d436d80'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Forces of Globalization', E'Forces of globalization have acted on education internationally, creating greater standardization in norms of teaching and learning. There has always been {{1}} similarity between schools across context: the curriculum, for example has been shown {{2}} between language, mathematics, science and the arts in a comparable way in schools around the world. Likewise, school buildings in terms of their architecture share remarkable similarities internationally, {{3}} of shared patterns of teaching and learning (although facilities and resources differ significantly.) In recent years, globalization has created increased opportunities for the ''borrowing'' of education policy and practice, and {{4}} further uniformity across national contexts. {{5}} has the internationalization of education been more apparent than in the higher education sector. The move to the internationalization of higher-education policy and practice {{6}} by increased student mobility, and the desire of universities to attract students from overseas.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.considerable, 2.to be divided, 3.as a result, 4.generated, 5.Nowhere, 6.was initially driven', NULL, NULL),
  ('1dcefc87-c46b-5d5f-8e1d-3292777bdba8'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RE_ORDER_PARAGRAPHS', E'RE_ORDER_PARAGRAPHS', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '1dcefc87-c46b-5d5f-8e1d-3292777bdba8'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Legless Man', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, E'5, 4, 2, 1, 3', NULL, NULL),
  ('1dd154fd-3db2-5a46-a395-de832214cd05'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '1dd154fd-3db2-5a46-a395-de832214cd05'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Teenage Daughter', E'Your teenage daughter gets top marks in school, captains the debate team, and volunteers at a shelter for homeless people. But while driving the family car, she text-messages her best friend and rear-ends another vehicle.\nHow can teens be so clever, accomplished, and responsible-and reckless {{1}} ? Easily, according to two physicians at Children''s Hospital Boston and Harvard Medical School (HMS) who have been {{2}} the unique structure and chemistry of the adolescent brain ."The teenage brain is not just an {{3}} brain with fewer miles on it," says Frances E. Jensen, a professor of neurology. "It''s a paradoxical time of {{4}} . These are people with very sharp brains, but they''re not quite sure what to do with them."\nResearch during the past 10 years, powered by technology such as functional magnetic resonance imaging, has revealed that young brains have {{5}} fast-growing synapses and sections that remain unconnected. This leaves teens easily influenced by their environment and more prone to impulsive behavior, even without the {{6}} of souped-up hormones and any genetic or family predispositions.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.at the same time, 2.exploring, 3.adult, 4.development, 5.both, 6.impact', NULL, NULL),
