  ('2d7668ad-109a-5a40-aced-fc826f46902d'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '2d7668ad-109a-5a40-aced-fc826f46902d'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'WFD', NULL, 'df7ae45b-d213-56c8-ae73-215499239925'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'A fire drill necessitates evacuation from buildings for everyone''s safety.', NULL, NULL),
  ('2d7d159e-5ab0-5343-87b0-d7de3cb0249d'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '2d7d159e-5ab0-5343-87b0-d7de3cb0249d'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'ASQ', E'What do we call the marine animal that has eight legs?', 'ddf00860-c000-5073-bfb8-957475092cb8'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Octopus', NULL, NULL),
  ('2d80fa48-a6aa-543c-a55e-c2031b5b8e7f'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RE_ORDER_PARAGRAPHS', E'RE_ORDER_PARAGRAPHS', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '2d80fa48-a6aa-543c-a55e-c2031b5b8e7f'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Accounting System', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, E'3, 1, 2, 4', NULL, NULL),
  ('2d9c30e4-a21d-5d19-97bb-5b403677ad84'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '2d9c30e4-a21d-5d19-97bb-5b403677ad84'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'ASQ', E'What is the smallest bone in the human body?', '159dcb30-78e9-5251-b15e-957f9ea42047'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Stapes', NULL, NULL),
  ('2d9f7573-e076-5a75-9334-413a5b35015b'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '2d9f7573-e076-5a75-9334-413a5b35015b'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Feeling of Fear', NULL, '65bcbe13-0f8e-5136-a7f0-5a6293c5ab8f'::uuid, NULL, E'We all get afraid and feel fear. Seeing a spider, a loud noise or a creak on floorboard late at night can strike fear suddenly right through our bodies. The feeling of fear can make your heart race, breath quicken, scream, sweat pupils dilate, freeze you in place and can even cause involuntary urination. These are all stress reactions caused by our limbic system a chain reaction in areas of the brain that work together to control a built-in ''fight-or-flight'' response. We have this built into us to help us react to and survive threats. If not for fear, we would most likely not have survived as a species. Lots of people actually seek out fear enjoying being and feeling scared. Watching horror films, playing scary games or even going on a roller-coaster. When our ''fight or flight'' response is triggered, we release chemicals which are similar to that of when we are excited or happy. When we trigger this in what we perceive as a safe environment, it is thought that we can then enjoy being scared and the chemicals running around our body that are akin to high arousal states.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('2da5c2d1-d73d-5f6e-89f1-b25f806d44e0'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '2da5c2d1-d73d-5f6e-89f1-b25f806d44e0'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'ASQ', E'How many years are there in two decades?', '5948559f-e420-5d6a-8cfc-1600abd9bb1a'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Twenty', NULL, NULL),
  ('2da5e8d2-26ad-57de-a932-774fe1b16a79'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'SUMMARIZE_WRITTEN_TEXT', E'SUMMARIZE_WRITTEN_TEXT', E'WRITING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '2da5e8d2-26ad-57de-a932-774fe1b16a79'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Academics', E'Current research into the nature of the relationship between participation in physical activity/sport and educational performance has produced mixed, inconsistent and often non-comparable results. For example, some cross-sectional studies illustrate a positive correlation between participation in sport and physical activity and academic success (e.g. maths, reading, acuity, reaction times). However, critics point to a general failure to solve the issue of direction of cause — whether intelligence leads to success in sport, whether involvement in sport enhances academic performance, or whether a third factor (e.g. personality traits) explains both.\nLongitudinal studies also generally support the suggestion that academic performance is enhanced, or at least maintained, by increased habitual physical activity. Yet such studies are criticized for not being definitive because some do not use randomised allocation of pupils to experimental and control groups (to control for pre-existing differences), others tend to use (subjective) teacher-assigned grades to assess academic achievement, rather than standardised and comparable tests; and some programmes include parallel interventions, making it difficult to isolate specific effects.\nMore generically, one key piece of research illustrates that both acute exercise and chronic training programmes have small, but beneficial, positive impacts on cognitive performance. However, this study concludes that as experimental rigour decreased, effect size increased. Further, generalisation is limited because effect size is influenced by the nature and type of exercise, the type of participants, the nature of the cognitive tests and the methodological quality of the study.', NULL, NULL, E'Current research into the nature of the relationship between participation in physical activity/sport and educational performance has produced mixed, inconsistent, and often non-comparable results, including conclusions that academic performance is enhanced or at least maintained, by increased habitual physical activity and that both acute exercise and chronic training programs have small, but beneficial, positive impacts on cognitive performance.', NULL, 5, 75),
  ('2da7c79b-7d34-5308-bd6c-5cb7ce06de35'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '2da7c79b-7d34-5308-bd6c-5cb7ce06de35'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'WFD', NULL, 'd4b0e1d7-1ae4-5736-9e71-1a2d51eb59e2'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Many university lectures can now be viewed on the Internet.', NULL, NULL),
  ('2daa05b6-9277-54a4-92f6-92357a298946'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '2daa05b6-9277-54a4-92f6-92357a298946'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Data Sharing', NULL, 'd589eb91-688a-5988-8b80-5b5adaf1cda4'::uuid, NULL, E'Organizations are far more likely to keep information on that attack to themselves. Why? Because they’re worried about competitive advantage, litigation or regulation. We need to effectively democratize threat intelligence data. We need to get all of these organizations to open up and share what is in their private arsenal of information. The bad guys are moving fast; we’ve got to move faster. And the best way to do that is to open up and share data on what’s happening. Let’s think about this in the construct of security professionals. Remember, they’re programmed right into their DNA to keep secrets. We’ve got to turn that thinking on its head. We’ve got to get governments, private institutions and security companies willing to share information at speed. And here’s why: because if you share the information, it’s equivalent to inoculation. And if you’re not sharing, you’re actually part of the problem, because you’re increasing the odds that other people could be impacted by the same attack techniques.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('2db51a8d-c2ad-5af8-ac5e-96121acf34d8'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '2db51a8d-c2ad-5af8-ac5e-96121acf34d8'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'WFD', NULL, '0ae4c439-3a91-5548-b202-3e5050c3b78f'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Professor Smith will be late for today''s lecture.', NULL, NULL),
  ('2dbbf5a0-7189-5efa-8e6f-f37010cb1c61'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_ESSAY', E'WRITE_ESSAY', E'WRITING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '2dbbf5a0-7189-5efa-8e6f-f37010cb1c61'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Practical Training', E'Some people think that universities should not provide so much theoretical knowledge but give more practical training throughout their courses. To what extent do you agree or disagree with this opinion?', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 200, 300),
