  ('3a916eae-57c1-59c7-b102-7302400afdd5'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '3a916eae-57c1-59c7-b102-7302400afdd5'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Innovative Teacher', E'Observing an innovative teacher in action can be enlightening. They might {{1}} to a shy student''s rare question with encouragement and depth, {{2}} sharply with their approach to more outspoken pupils. Such teachers are adept at tailoring their responses to {{3}} out the best in each student, {{4}} a keen awareness and adaptability.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.respond, 2.contrasting, 3.draw, 4.demonstrating', NULL, NULL),
  ('3a947969-7203-5c1d-b114-8c7d68aa84b2'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RE_ORDER_PARAGRAPHS', E'RE_ORDER_PARAGRAPHS', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '3a947969-7203-5c1d-b114-8c7d68aa84b2'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Running of Bulls', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, E'3, 1, 4, 2', NULL, NULL),
  ('3a9522e6-ce71-5bb8-b00a-8200fa883edb'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'HIGHLIGHT_CORRECT_SUMMARY', E'HIGHLIGHT_CORRECT_SUMMARY', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '3a9522e6-ce71-5bb8-b00a-8200fa883edb'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Pancake Ice', E'Pancake ice is an intermediate step between an open-water configuration and a totally frozen body of water. We know that a combination of very cold temperatures and waves is necessary to make the pancakes form in the first place. After these waves make it form, there''s an entire spectrum - so a wide collection of frequency waves that they would encounter, whether they be from wind shear or they''d be from you know that the ocean long slow waves. The easiest way to study it is to look at each individual range of frequencies one after another. Sort of gain a wider perspective on how the pancake ice interacts with waves. The ultimate aim of our research is to better inform the meteorological modelers of the world who are looking at Antarctica as well as the Arctic and some of the other cold regions of the world. We''re conducting work in the Sea-Ice-Wind-Wave-lnteraction facility here at UniMeIb. It was designed by the head of our department Jason Monty. He had the foresight to use a modular design, which means there are individual sections that are stacked together, so since we''re built to the space that we have in this lab right now. When we moved to Fishermans Bend we can extend our model and a few more sections to make it much longer. And that will enable us to have longer runtimes, have more developed waves as well as add some other possibilities of study.\n\nClick on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', '94b07f7c-d586-5ac1-b573-37e112111060'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Pancake ice is formed by extremely cold temperature and waves, which needs a wide collection of frequency. The aim of the research is to give the meteorological modelers a better understanding of this phenomenon through a special lab.', NULL, NULL),
  ('3a9e6c6c-c03c-5a76-a34d-71ce945124ca'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '3a9e6c6c-c03c-5a76-a34d-71ce945124ca'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Mercury Subsurface', E'A new study published in the journal Astrobiology suggests that the subsurface of Mercury, the hottest and nearest planet to the sun, could {{1}} life. The researchers used data from NASA’s MESSENGER mission, which orbited Mercury from 2011 to 2015, to analyze the geology and chemistry of the planet’s northern hemisphere. They found evidence of salt flow in the Raditladi and Eminescu basins, which are large impact craters. These salt formations are similar to those found in extreme {{2}} on Earth, such as the Atacama Desert in Chile. The study proposes that these subsurface briny ice layers, protected from the harsh surface conditions by a thick layer of rock, could provide a suitable environment for life to {{3}} . The discovery challenges the conventional view that Mercury is too hot and dry {{4}} life, and expands the potential for finding life on other planets with hot star-like features. The study also highlights the importance of exploring the subsurface regions of Mercury and other planetary bodies, as they may {{5}} more secrets than their barren surfaces suggest.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.harbor, 2.habitats, 3.exist, 4.to support, 5.hold', NULL, NULL),
  ('3a9fc747-9ebf-5dc9-83ac-6f653944d0d0'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '3a9fc747-9ebf-5dc9-83ac-6f653944d0d0'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'WFD', NULL, '4409b70d-1da5-5ba3-9f80-199b9ac62d04'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Consumers are better informed today because of the Internet.', NULL, NULL),
