  ('3f67ddae-f92b-53d8-96a4-fa7be7ab9045'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '3f67ddae-f92b-53d8-96a4-fa7be7ab9045'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'RS', NULL, '04235263-03d8-5912-a55d-177c467bc5cc'::uuid, NULL, E'A key feature in drug development is examination of the pharmacological effects.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('3f67e948-f32f-5ba2-a01b-2ef5d87fa268'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '3f67e948-f32f-5ba2-a01b-2ef5d87fa268'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Anxiety', E'Anxiety can be, at times, truly {{1}} , making it hard to go about your day. However, feelings of unease, worry and fear – {{2}} mild or more severe – can be dealt with. In the moment of an anxiety {{3}} or any feelings of uneasiness, there are ways to {{4}} back and bring yourself back to a more comfortable feeling.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.debilitating, 2.whether, 3.attack, 4.step', NULL, NULL),
  ('3f6bb540-a53c-50cb-b840-7d1064de88af'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'SUMMARIZE_WRITTEN_TEXT', E'SUMMARIZE_WRITTEN_TEXT', E'WRITING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '3f6bb540-a53c-50cb-b840-7d1064de88af'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Near Infra-red', E'The colors that we see are a result of the light reflected within a narrow range of wavelengths – what we call the visible spectrum, but sunlight also spans wavelengths that we cannot see. Humans can''t see ultraviolet wavelengths, which many other animals can see, but there''s one set of wavelengths that elude all of us – these are near infra-red (NIR) wavelengths. Understanding how bird feathers interact with these wavelengths is important, not just for birds, but also for humans through the potential for improvements in thermal efficiency. Our research in the School of BioSciences at the University of Melbourne suggests that some Australian birds can control their temperature and avoid overheating by reflecting near-infrared wavelengths of sunlight. We collected information on 90 species of Australian birds and found a very strong link between living in hot, arid regions and reflecting a higher proportion of near-infrared light. Researchers in the field of animal colouration have largely ignored near-infrared light, because it isn''t easy to measure and there''s no evidence that animals can see these wavelengths. Because these wavelengths are invisible, they don''t affect camouflage or sexual attractiveness, which are very important in the animal world. This means that many animals can control their temperature by altering reflection of near-infrared light without compromising their ability to hide or attract a mate.', NULL, NULL, E'Humans can''t see ultraviolet wavelengths, which many other animals can see, but there''s one set of wavelengths that elude all of us — these are near infra-red (NIR) wavelengths, and understanding how bird feathers interact with these wavelengths is important for humans through the potential for improvements in thermal efficiency, since many animals can control their temperature by altering the reflection of near-infrared light without compromising their ability to hide or attract a mate.', NULL, 5, 75),
  ('3f6dfb70-3057-597a-addd-c0fceb9499d3'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RE_ORDER_PARAGRAPHS', E'RE_ORDER_PARAGRAPHS', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '3f6dfb70-3057-597a-addd-c0fceb9499d3'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'American Regionalism Movement', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, E'4, 3, 1, 2', NULL, NULL),
  ('3f7ce201-43be-50e4-a47a-3ef614888c1c'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '3f7ce201-43be-50e4-a47a-3ef614888c1c'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Moving', E'For every action, there''s a {{1}} . And for many movements we make, there''s an {{2}} : we think about moving, and we move. Now a study published in the May 8th issue of the journal Science suggests that the experience of moving is all in your mind. Because the part of the brain that''s active when you intend to move is the same part that lets you feel like you did.\nTwo separate brain regions are involved in moving your body. One part provides the intention, and the other powers the actual movement. But researchers didn''t know which part let you know that you actually moved.\nIn the new study, scientists were working with patients {{5}} surgery to remove a brain tumor. Surgeons often electrically {{6}} the area around the tumor while the patient is awake and can provide feedback, so they can avoid damaging critical tissue. The scientists found that zapping one {{7}} part of the brain made their patients feel like they wanted to move their arms, lips or tongue. And ramping up the stimulation to that spot made them feel like they''d done it. But when the team poked at the region that actually caused motion, the patients didn''t know they moved—a finding that''s oddly moving.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.reaction, 2.intention, 3.undergoing, 4.stimulate, 5.particular', NULL, NULL),
  ('3f7d7062-fa23-5d43-a1f4-bfa685ec1490'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '3f7d7062-fa23-5d43-a1f4-bfa685ec1490'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'ASQ', E'What is the device that can show directions and has a needle that always points south?', '1fce7335-c257-5dc8-b214-4e29b2a3d4fb'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Compass', NULL, NULL),
