  ('40ac1a40-d112-57ac-b598-ddd80e8dff36'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '40ac1a40-d112-57ac-b598-ddd80e8dff36'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Employee Diversity', E'Differences fuel battles and even wars. That’s true in the political arena, and it can also be true in the workplace. Problems that can arise with a {{1}} workplace include difficulty with communication, different work styles or work ethics. Small issues, such as different smells in the lunchroom, can quickly {{2}} if not managed appropriately. Differences in culture can also lead to miscommunication. For example, Americans value eye contact — even with members of the opposite sex. But in many Asian and Middle Eastern countries, direct eye contact is considered to be disrespectful. Direct eye contact with someone of the opposite sex may even be seen as flirting. It’s very important, therefore, to provide diversity training to avoid serious social {{3}} and challenges. Work styles can also vary from culture to culture. Although many Asians and Central Americans work in groups and value consensus, most Germans and Americans prefer to work independently. Some cultures place a high value on order, organization, and method whereas others emphasize {{4}} and flexible thinking.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.diverse, 2.escalate, 3.gaffes, 4.spontaneity', NULL, NULL),
  ('40b05593-e847-522d-b756-4c7461c73169'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_ESSAY', E'WRITE_ESSAY', E'WRITING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '40b05593-e847-522d-b756-4c7461c73169'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Complaint', E'When people need to complain about a product or a service, some prefer to complain in writing while others prefer to complain in person. Which way do you prefer?', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 200, 300),
  ('40b83069-eb90-58a3-bbd4-6ff492a2c649'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '40b83069-eb90-58a3-bbd4-6ff492a2c649'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'ASQ', E'What is the object that has six square sides?', '0db35471-dc65-5d72-981f-b1d9d0b39d4b'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Cube', NULL, NULL),
  ('40b8e957-18ed-5c01-ab75-195c1dab9b14'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '40b8e957-18ed-5c01-ab75-195c1dab9b14'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Learning by Doing', E'An analysis of more than 500 graduates found no significant difference between business schools that offered traditional courses and those that emphasise a ''learning-by-doing'' approach to entrepreneurship education.\nThe research challenges the ongoing trend across higher education institutes (HEIs) of {{1}} on experiential learning, and suggests that universities need to reconsider their approach if they {{2}} increase entrepreneurship among their students.\nMs Inna Kozlinska, research associate at Aston Business School and author of the study, said: ''Entrepreneurship education {{3}} as a major force capable of generating long-term socio-economic changes through developing entrepreneurial, creative, flexible and wise individuals. There is an ongoing shift towards experiential learning in business schools, {{4}} there is little empirical evidence to suggest this approach has better impact than traditional learning.''', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.focusing, 2.are to, 3.is seen, 4.yet', NULL, NULL),
  ('40bddd1b-6fef-5ed0-819a-bf0162fc530f'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '40bddd1b-6fef-5ed0-819a-bf0162fc530f'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Commercial Ads', E'From a child''s point of view, what is the purpose of TV advertising? Is advertising on TV done to give actors the opportunity to take a rest or {{1}} their lines? Or is it done to make people buy things? Furthermore, is the main {{2}} between programs and commercials that commercials are for real, whereas programs are not, or that programs are for kids and commercials for {{3}} ? As has been shown several times in the literature (e.g. Butter et al 1981 Donohue, Henke, and Donohue 1980 Macklin 1983 and 1987 Robertson and Rossiter 1974 Stephens and Stutts 1982), some children are able to {{4}} between programs and commercials and are {{5}} of the intent of TV advertising, whereas others are not.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.practice, 2.difference, 3.adults, 4.distinguish, 5.aware', NULL, NULL),
  ('40c0e574-bffa-5a58-b79a-30ccc8890b13'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'READ_ALOUD', E'READ_ALOUD', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '40c0e574-bffa-5a58-b79a-30ccc8890b13'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Colonial Institution', E'The colonial institution has a lengthy and illustrious history that dates back over 200 years. Many of the persons who have worked here are scientists themselves, such as Albert Einstein. He produced breakthroughs that made it simpler to produce and use electricity, allowing us to do things like switch on lights, cook dinner, and play video games on consoles.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
