  ('426c08f0-d485-5a29-bb84-d5f394efb5f2'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '426c08f0-d485-5a29-bb84-d5f394efb5f2'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'ASQ', E'What does ''subterranean'' mean?', 'e4c227e2-f978-55ff-8a51-07a4321f0ecb'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Underground', NULL, NULL),
  ('426f21a9-e7e6-54dd-99ad-e2727d26af5a'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '426f21a9-e7e6-54dd-99ad-e2727d26af5a'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'WFD', NULL, '69bd28ca-d1be-535d-8071-edfb55a244b3'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'The employment rate steadily increased over the last year.', NULL, NULL),
  ('42701f8d-da4a-5abb-8205-b8d64b5a85a3'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'MC_READING_SINGLE', E'MC_READING_SINGLE', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '42701f8d-da4a-5abb-8205-b8d64b5a85a3'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Artist’s Inspiration', E'Being one of his best works and he never again allowed his art to be influenced by contemporary politics. On the contrary, he had the utmost contempt for the age in which he lived, for its crass materialism and complacent belief in progress; and his art is almost entirely an attempt to escape from it. He escaped into the subjects of romantic poetry, in particular, that of Shakespeare, Byron, and Walter Scott. Some of his greatest pictures were inspired by Byron, and he had a Byronic power of self-identification with the forces of the sublime - in particular ‘the roaring of lions and the destructive sword’. Baudelaire said that when an idea shot through his mind his muscles quivered with impatience and his eyes shone like a tiger stalking its prey. At feeding time in the Paris Zoo, he was overwhelmed with happiness.\n\nWhat can be inferred from this passage?', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'The artist’s inspiration came from his reflection.', NULL, NULL),
  ('4276b943-3c16-59ae-a84c-ca0c6dfe6ebc'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4276b943-3c16-59ae-a84c-ca0c6dfe6ebc'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Approach to Sanitation', NULL, '2b05cccf-1198-562b-ae9a-e3037aefc207'::uuid, NULL, E'We can fertilize our food. Each one of us is pooping and peeing something that could fertilize half or maybe all of our food, depending on our diet. That dark brown poo in the toilet is dark brown because of what? Dead stuff, bacteria. That’s carbon. And carbon, if we’re getting that into the soil, is going to bind to the other minerals and nutrients in there. Boom! Healthier food. Voilà! Healthier people. Chemical fertilizers by definition don’t have carbon in them. Imagine if we could move our animal manure and our human manure to our soil, we might not need to rely on fossil fuel-based fertilizers, mine minerals from far away. Imagine how much energy we could save. Now, some of us are concerned about industrial pollutants contaminating this reuse cycle. That can be addressed. But we need to separate our discomfort about talking about poo and pee so we can calmly talk about how we want to reuse it and what things we don’t want to reuse. And get this: if we change our approach to sanitation, we can start to slow down climate change. Remember that carbon in the poop? If we can get that into our soil bank, it’s going to start to absorb carbon dioxide that we put into the air. And that could help slow down global warming.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('42787a01-0580-59eb-bea8-975e1460f6b6'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '42787a01-0580-59eb-bea8-975e1460f6b6'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Octopus', E'If consciousness comes in degrees, then how far along on the spectrum is the octopus? Octopuses almost certainly feel pain. They nurse and protect {{1}} body parts, and show a preference not to be touched near wounds. In addition to feeling pain, octopuses also have {{2}} sensory capacities: excellent eyesight, and acute sensitivity to taste and smell. This, together with their large nervous systems and {{3}} behavior makes it all but certain. The question of what subjective experience might be like for an octopus is {{4}} by the odd relationship between its brain and body.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.injured, 2.sophisticated, 3.complex, 4.complicated', NULL, NULL),
  ('4279be58-00b6-5bc7-8d0e-f8f39b9292d5'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RESPOND_TO_A_SITUATION', E'RESPOND_TO_A_SITUATION', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4279be58-00b6-5bc7-8d0e-f8f39b9292d5'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Dessert', E'You invite some friends to your party, and one of them wants to bring some dessert. You have already prepared the dessert. So you hope your friend can bring something else (for example: fruit, cake, etc..). What should you say to your friend?', '3c05a554-76b4-5b35-8cfb-49b217b8ad76'::uuid, NULL, E'Hey friend, I want to talk to you about something.\nWe seem to have a little issue with food for my party.\nIt''s like I’ve already prepared all the desserts, so could you bring some fruit instead?\nAnd I''d like to know what you think.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('427c70f7-e48d-5d30-a68a-2b1e4aa97f13'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '427c70f7-e48d-5d30-a68a-2b1e4aa97f13'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'RS', NULL, '77e88ed6-fab2-51c2-8c55-bfcf23a7029c'::uuid, NULL, E'Many species have not yet been discovered by biologists.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('42822d31-937a-5e21-8a99-9db07e28a6e8'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '42822d31-937a-5e21-8a99-9db07e28a6e8'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Emotional Theme Park', E'Theme parks are more than just ‘fun’ places to visit. Theme parks are safe environments where guests can temporarily {{1}} themselves into instances and situations they may not {{2}} in their day-to-day lives. Instances and situations may push their persona comfort levels such as dark rides where special effects {{3}} the immersive experience, or shows and films that may {{4}} personal feelings of happiness, sadness, and exhilaration.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.immerse, 2.experience, 3.enhance, 4.evoke', NULL, NULL),
  ('4288147d-0de2-5420-b95b-43ad65a839db'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'READ_ALOUD', E'READ_ALOUD', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4288147d-0de2-5420-b95b-43ad65a839db'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Malaria Vaccine', E'The World Health Organisation said an experimental malaria vaccine will be tested on more than 750,000 children in Ghana, Kenya and Malawi next year. The disease causes more than 400,000 deaths annually. How the vaccine is implemented will be key to its success: it must be administered four times, with the final dose 18 months after the third.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('4289ca75-149d-54c2-9bad-5c43d5aeea43'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'SUMMARIZE_WRITTEN_TEXT', E'SUMMARIZE_WRITTEN_TEXT', E'WRITING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4289ca75-149d-54c2-9bad-5c43d5aeea43'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Office Environment', E'The notion that office space has a role in promoting or inhibiting performance is backed up by solid research. A recent study conducted by Harvard University and Massachusetts General Hospital showed that improvements to the physical surroundings of workers impacted on productivity not just because the working environment was more attractive, but because the changes made employees feel cared for. A Swedish research paper revealed a strong link between the type of office an employee worked in and their overall job satisfaction and health. Various findings have emerged as a result of studies such as this. Pot plants and greenery can apparently have a real impact on psychological well-being. Those who work in a private room tend to be in better health than workers based in open-plan offices.\nSufficient light can reduce sickness among workers and increase productivity, and an attractive office can make workers feel more cared for and therefore more loyal to their company. Most of these points make good rational sense. But some companies aren''t content simply to increase the health , productivity and contentment of their employees. Pioneers such as Google, Walt Disney and Dyson have tried to create offices that will do everything from promoting collaboration between workers to stimulating their creative juices. "Environment, both physical and cultural, can make or break creativity." says Kursty Groves, author of I Wish I Worked There! A look inside the most creative spaces in business. Stimulating spaces expose the mind to a variety of stimuli - planned or random - In order to encourage people to think differently. Reflective spaces promote the filtering of information into the brain slowing it to make connections. An environment which encourages a team to build trust and to play freely is an essential ingredient for innovation.', NULL, NULL, E'The notion that office space has a role in promoting or inhibiting performance is backed up by solid research, and improvements to the physical surroundings of workers impact on productivity, while sufficient light can reduce sickness among workers and increase productivity, and an attractive office can make workers feel more loyal to their company, so an environment which encourages a team to build trust and to play freely is an essential ingredient for innovation.', NULL, 5, 75),
  ('428bbf20-63cc-533a-9808-dc758c46d40c'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '428bbf20-63cc-533a-9808-dc758c46d40c'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'RS', NULL, '41cd9494-31c3-501d-92f3-cd85c6f53ccb'::uuid, NULL, E'Even without someone to converse with, Mary doesn''t experience loneliness.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
