  ('445bdb40-8752-5868-b4e9-923511b83421'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '445bdb40-8752-5868-b4e9-923511b83421'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'RS', NULL, 'b3549b4b-4694-5e90-85a9-8e6c93fa7b29'::uuid, NULL, E'Despite using English, I failed to communicate with him effectively.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('445cf53b-778f-5491-93bf-e67fe57932d3'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '445cf53b-778f-5491-93bf-e67fe57932d3'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'ASQ', E'How many continents are there in Africa?', '7066a943-f934-59b0-992d-40fa264ae2ef'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'One', NULL, NULL),
  ('445d7889-9c43-5707-bb71-872f7fcb3613'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '445d7889-9c43-5707-bb71-872f7fcb3613'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'RS', NULL, '1b05b9f0-8fb0-57a3-b9ce-bd68c77a899d'::uuid, NULL, E'There will be a conference here next summer on using the web for academic research.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('445de51a-a917-52db-bf72-44ee1679c974'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '445de51a-a917-52db-bf72-44ee1679c974'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Stem Cells', NULL, 'e799e59b-4c56-5b11-b87c-034e57e146d4'::uuid, NULL, E'Stem cells are cells that are undifferentiated, meaning they do not have a specific job or function. While skin cells protect your body, muscle cells contract and nerve cells send signals, stem cells do not have any specific structures or functions.\nStem cells do have the potential to become all other kinds of cells in your body. Your body uses stem cells to replace worn-out cells when they die. For example, you completely replace the lining of your intestines every four days. Stem cells beneath the lining of your intestines replace these cells as they wear out. Scientists hope that stem cells could be used to create a very special kind of personalized medicine, in which we could replace your own body parts with, well, your own body parts.\nStem cell researchers are working hard to find ways in which to use stem cells to create new tissue to replace the parts of organs that are damaged by injury or disease. Using stem cells to replace damaged bodily tissue is called regenerative medicine. For example, scientists currently use stem cells to treat patients with blood diseases such as leukemia. Leukemia is a form of cancer that affects your bone marrow. Bone marrow is the spongy tissue inside your bones where your blood cells are created. In leukemia, some of the cells inside your bone marrow grow uncontrollably, crowding out the healthy stem cells that form your blood cells. Some leukemia patients can receive a stem cell transplant. These new stem cells will create the blood cells needed by the patient''s body.\nThere are actually multiple kinds of stem cells that scientists can use for medical treatments and research. Adult stem cells or tissue-specific stem cells are found in small numbers in most of your body''s tissues.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('446339e8-eeee-5904-818b-46bd881f9220'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '446339e8-eeee-5904-818b-46bd881f9220'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'RS', NULL, '304ed745-35a3-5eb0-95db-416ac2fd2149'::uuid, NULL, E'She used to be everywhere, but today she is missing.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('4467d856-fdb7-56fd-a707-6c3cb5856eb2'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'READ_ALOUD', E'READ_ALOUD', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4467d856-fdb7-56fd-a707-6c3cb5856eb2'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Low-oxygen Sites', E'In the past 50 years, the amount of water in the open ocean with zero oxygen has gone up more than fourfold. In coastal water bodies, including estuaries and seas, low-oxygen sites have increased more than tenfold since 1950. Scientists expect oxygen to continue dropping even outside these zones as Earth warms.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('4469ede4-ebe7-59e0-8ca3-ae117b774b1f'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4469ede4-ebe7-59e0-8ca3-ae117b774b1f'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Soap', NULL, '6ea09753-417a-57e5-9283-827073b60c74'::uuid, NULL, E'Okay, as you look at this next exhibit, you''ll notice something quite common—an ordinary bar of soap. Now, soap has been around a long time. In fact, the ancient Phoenicians produced soap as a substance for washing the body way back in 600 B.C. They made it by blending goat''s fat with wood ash. The Phoenicians, as you may know, regularly traded along the Mediterranean, and they were the ones who introduced soap to the Greeks and Romans. Now, soap was not something welcomed by all countries. In fact, during the superstitious Middle Ages, many people were afraid to bathe their whole body too frequently. They thought it could be dangerous for their health—that it could even kill them. And even after the production of soap became common in some European countries in the eleventh and twelfth centuries, even then some people in the heart of Europe refused to use it. You''ll find it interesting that when a duchess was given a box of soap as a present in 1549, she was so insulted that she had the gift-giver thrown off her estate! But by the nineteenth century the attitude toward soap had changed drastically. In some regions of Europe the tax on soap was so high that people secretly made their own. A baron went so far as to suggest that the wealth of a nation could be judged according to the amount of soap it used. Now, if you turn your attention to the next exhibit, you can see how soap is manufactured today.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('446b2556-e15c-5a4d-b045-67840584d933'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '446b2556-e15c-5a4d-b045-67840584d933'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Social Media', NULL, 'a2dbd465-8670-577e-bdce-3d013c4566b4'::uuid, NULL, E'In the digital age, the influence of ''Social Media'' on public opinion is both profound and unprecedented. Platforms like Twitter and Facebook have transformed the landscape of communication, becoming the modern-day town squares where opinions are formed and voiced. First, social media breaks down barriers to information dissemination. It allows for the rapid spread of information, enabling anyone with an internet connection to stay updated on global events. This democratization of information empowers individuals, but it also requires a critical eye to navigate the vast sea of content and distinguish between fact and fiction. Second, social media has a unique power to mobilize. It can unite individuals around causes, as seen in movements like #MeToo, which gained momentum through viral social media campaigns, highlighting issues that may otherwise receive limited attention in traditional media outlets. As we engage with social media, let''s be mindful of its power to shape our perceptions and the importance of fostering digital literacy to navigate this new public sphere responsibly.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('446d0845-7e76-5ca4-b7a7-77e799158441'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '446d0845-7e76-5ca4-b7a7-77e799158441'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'RS', NULL, 'c96dfae3-610a-5de0-a7a3-d1a4f48a2ede'::uuid, NULL, E'Scientists still aren''t sure why we need to sleep.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('446d143e-0fa7-51df-a744-0b949b16c47f'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '446d143e-0fa7-51df-a744-0b949b16c47f'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'WFD', NULL, '0cd84b2d-55e9-515e-b8d3-75158d0c4780'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Collaborative projects help students develop teamwork and problem-solving abilities.', NULL, NULL),
  ('44768d04-4647-5081-83a9-3eeac1c32c04'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '44768d04-4647-5081-83a9-3eeac1c32c04'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Absent-mindedness', E'It seems you always forget — your reading glasses when you are rushing to work, your coat when you are going to the cleaners, your credit card when you are shopping. Such absent-mindedness may be {{1}} to you; now British and German scientists are developing memory glasses that record everything the {{2}} sees. The glasses can play back memories later to help the wearer remember things they have forgotten such as where they left their keys. And the glasses also {{3}} the user to "label" items so that information can be used later on. The wearer could walk around an office or a factory identifying certain {{4}} by pointing at them. Objects indicated are then given a {{5}} label on a screen inside the glasses that the user then fills in.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.frustrating, 2.user, 3.allow, 4.items, 5.blank', NULL, NULL),
  ('4478e0c7-4945-53d8-a9d5-95fcb9229ad9'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4478e0c7-4945-53d8-a9d5-95fcb9229ad9'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Childcare', E'Affordable early years education and childcare potentially enables parents, particularly mothers, to be in paid employment. International studies have found that countries with greater enrolment rates in publicly funded or provided childcare also have higher maternal employment rates, {{1}} untangling causal relationships is complex. From the point of view of the household, additional income, especially for the less well-off, is itself associated with better outcomes for children, as child poverty has been shown to be a key independent determinant of children''s outcomes. And, {{2}} the point of view of the public purse, as mothers {{3}} employment they are likely to claim fewer benefits and to generate extra revenues {{4}} income tax and national insurance.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.although, 2.from, 3.enter, 4.through', NULL, NULL),
  ('447e5948-ab7a-5896-bef0-d210d89d11a9'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '447e5948-ab7a-5896-bef0-d210d89d11a9'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Fabrication', E'Fabrication is the falsification of data, information, or citations in an academic assignment, including making up citations to {{1}} up arguments or inventing quotations. Fabrication is most common in the natural sciences where students sometimes {{2}} data to make experiments “work” or false {{3}} are made about the research performed.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.back, 2.falsify, 3.claims', NULL, NULL),
  ('44857bb3-ae02-5874-840c-e7cc32a72d3a'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '44857bb3-ae02-5874-840c-e7cc32a72d3a'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Garden', E'When a neighborhood has a well-maintained garden, it becomes more desirable. People see these gardens and think, "This is a nice place to live." It''s not just about the greenery but also about what it represents – a community that cares, that''s active and {{1}} . But it''s not just about making the area look better. These gardens can become central points in a neighborhood, places where people meet and build relationships. They create a sense of community that''s hard to find in urban {{2}} . In a garden, you might meet your neighbors for the first time, share gardening tips, or even swap stories. It''s a bonding {{3}} , one that strengthens community ties. Community gardens are not just plots of land for growing vegetables and flowers. They''re spaces where community spirit {{4}} root and grows. They encourage people to take {{5}} in their neighborhood, foster environmental responsibility, and even contribute to local food production. In a way, these gardens are a reflection of the community''s health and vitality – where the garden thrives, so does the neighborhood.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.engaged, 2.settings, 3.experience, 4.takes, 5.pride', NULL, NULL),
  ('44886db6-ff6e-5295-82d7-d1570e5ffcb4'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '44886db6-ff6e-5295-82d7-d1570e5ffcb4'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'WFD', NULL, '1be7e623-41a5-5deb-9fc1-d8b9c911e640'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'The university is highly rated for its research accuracy and ability.', NULL, NULL),
  ('448a3cb5-2ab9-51e9-b816-2140f3ff068a'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_ESSAY', E'WRITE_ESSAY', E'WRITING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '448a3cb5-2ab9-51e9-b816-2140f3ff068a'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Children''s Decisions', E'Parents or other adult relatives should make important decisions for their older (15 to 18 year-old) teenage children. To what extent do you agree with this statement?', NULL, NULL, E'The question of whether parents or other adult relatives should make important decisions for their older teenage children is a complex one. While some argue that teenagers need to learn independence and decision-making skills, I believe that parents or other adult relatives should make important decisions for their older teenage children because teenagers lack the life experience and maturity to make important decisions, parents or other adult relatives have the best interests of their children at heart, and making important decisions can be stressful for teenagers.\nTeenagers lack the life experience and maturity to make important decisions. While teenagers may have strong opinions and desires, they may not fully understand the consequences of their decisions. Parents or other adult relatives have more life experience and can provide guidance and support to help teenagers make the best decisions.\nParents or other adult relatives have the best interests of their children at heart. They want their children to be happy, healthy, and successful, and will make decisions that align with those goals. Teenagers may not always know what is best for them, but parents or other adult relatives have a broader perspective and can make decisions that benefit their children in the long run.\nIn conclusion, I believe that parents or other adult relatives should make important decisions for their older teenage children. While some may argue that teenagers need to learn independence and decision-making skills, it is important to balance that with guidance and support from trusted adults. By working together, parents or other adult relatives can help their teenage children make important decisions that will benefit them in the long run.', NULL, 200, 300),
  ('448e6b1e-9c84-5e5d-9cf2-b5c5421d4aaa'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'MC_LISTENING_SINGLE', E'MC_LISTENING_SINGLE', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '448e6b1e-9c84-5e5d-9cf2-b5c5421d4aaa'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Roman Doctors', E'Listen to part of a lecture and answer the questions. Okay, class today we will talk about Roman doctors at the beginning of the first century. As we noted in our previous class. There was a fifteen year long war after julius caesar was assassinated. The war was severe, the number of injured was so many. There were so many that it became one of the top priorities of the new emperor. To give medical care to those in need. It was around this time that the new emperor augustus started thinking about upgrading the status of doctors. He realized that medical care was key to the empire, and especially an army. In order to improve the medical system, he needed better doctors. So he started making the profession look more enticing. All army doctors were entitled to attend the new army medical school and were given dignified titles, land grants and special retirement benefits. Before this, doctors had a fairly low status.\n\nWhy did Roman doctors attend the army''s medical school according to the lecture?', 'bb2052cc-c0f0-5ebe-8ccb-bb5c010c3e10'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'They were keys to the army.', NULL, NULL),
