  ('452ccdee-a114-5fd1-a961-a5f8347d5060'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '452ccdee-a114-5fd1-a961-a5f8347d5060'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'WFD', NULL, 'ace9f39c-0cc1-5f21-bf16-26ce25ad896c'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'He''s writing a history of the town for a local publisher.', NULL, NULL),
  ('452e5708-0fe9-5ea4-9003-9a57bbe501c3'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'MC_LISTENING_MULTIPLE', E'MC_LISTENING_MULTIPLE', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '452e5708-0fe9-5ea4-9003-9a57bbe501c3'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Re-adjusting to Life', E'Re-adjusting to life in your own country after living abroad for some time is a little like recovering from jet-lag after a long flight across several time zones. It takes time. And research indicates that after nine years living in a foreign country you never really do re-adjust. Of course, things have changed: governments have come and gone, what you knew as countryside has become a suburb, new technologies have changed the way people go about their daily lives, and so on. These changes may well have been taking place in your adopted country, but they were happening while you were there, so you could adapt as you went along. Those are nor the main difficulties, however. It is in the smaller, everyday things that you might experience what is known as culture shock, although it''s not really a shock. but puzzling all the same. For example, the precise way to behave at a supermarket checkout may have changed. And in ordinary conversation, the frames of reference have changed, and quite often you find that you don''t really know what people are talking about, even though they are speaking your native tongue.\n\nWhich of the following are mentioned as being difficult to re-adapt to when returning to your native country after a long absence?', '7fe99b41-af9f-5afa-9bb8-c21481ca17fb'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'The way people go about their daily lives\nPeople''s terms of reference in conversation', NULL, NULL),
  ('45319cb5-5d94-5c06-a099-7c07a2d4e398'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '45319cb5-5d94-5c06-a099-7c07a2d4e398'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'ASQ', E'How do we describe two lines that are the same distance apart along their whole lengths and do not meet at any point?', '42d9cee1-50cd-5c0f-98a7-6f7e00bcfdea'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Parallel', NULL, NULL),
  ('4533320e-5f82-524c-96b6-29e437bbd147'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4533320e-5f82-524c-96b6-29e437bbd147'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'WFD', NULL, '25d44e34-e368-5983-896e-f3e83d2875ac'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Background music can help students concentrate on their studies.', NULL, NULL),
  ('4536d7bf-7065-5c73-a0e1-0b3c08102e2f'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4536d7bf-7065-5c73-a0e1-0b3c08102e2f'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'ASQ', E'What is the capital of Mexico?', '2e47c701-cb8d-59dc-9385-f56ae00917ae'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Mexico City', NULL, NULL),
  ('453905a7-29ee-5cde-90f4-18afe7f1f225'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '453905a7-29ee-5cde-90f4-18afe7f1f225'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Reading', E'Reading is an active process, not a {{1}} one. We always read within a {{2}} context, and this affects what we notice and what seems to matter. We always have a purpose in reading a text, and this will shape how we {{3}} it. Our purpose and background knowledge will also {{4}} the strategies we use to read the text.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.passive, 2.specific, 3.approach, 4.determine', NULL, NULL),
  ('45396b8d-a983-5214-a568-397e264a6c6d'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '45396b8d-a983-5214-a568-397e264a6c6d'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Treating Obesity', E'There are ways to prevent and treat obesity, such as lifestyle interventions, where families learn about and participate in healthy habits together. These interventions can help families improve their nutrition, physical activity, sleep, and stress {{1}} . However, many families do not have {{2}} to these interventions, due to various barriers, such as cost, availability, transportation, time, and stigma. These barriers are often unfair and reflect the social and economic {{3}} in our society. They prevent many families from getting the help they need and deserve. One example of this is the story of a pediatrician {{4}} a mother find healthy activities for her 10-year-old daughter who had pre-diabetes. The pediatrician faced many challenges and frustrations in finding suitable options for the family, and realized that the system was not working for them. She {{5}} that there were more resources and opportunities for families like them to learn about health as a family, to play, and to thrive.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.management, 2.access, 3.inequalities, 4.who helped, 5.wished', NULL, NULL),
  ('453c84ac-8844-53f2-b0f6-8ed214278de4'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '453c84ac-8844-53f2-b0f6-8ed214278de4'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'ASQ', E'What do we call a person who is single?', 'dfee47ae-260f-5ace-8221-b8dc61d1363f'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Singleton', NULL, NULL),
  ('4542b4dc-8d88-5d73-9f90-ede8bee9cc0f'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4542b4dc-8d88-5d73-9f90-ede8bee9cc0f'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Enlightments', E'Of course there were many different Enlightenments, and scholars still argue about which was the real torch-bearer. However, despite their quarrelsome diversity, most Enlightenment thinkers shared certain intellectual traits - a / an {{1}} on intellectual autonomy, a {{2}} of tradition and authority as the infallible sources of truth, a {{3}} of bigotry and persecution, a {{4}} to free enquiry, a {{5}} that (in Francis Bacon''s words) knowledge is power.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.insistence, 2.rejection, 3.dislike, 4.commitment, 5.belief', NULL, NULL),
  ('4543ba04-61b4-549c-b916-db699a402a72'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4543ba04-61b4-549c-b916-db699a402a72'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'ASQ', E'What do you call students in universities who have not yet graduated?', '4e0ea85e-925d-5158-88dc-3844d495f120'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Undergraduates', NULL, NULL),
  ('4546d57f-b9c6-51c0-9e22-8a9bb7c19de5'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'MC_READING_MULTIPLE', E'MC_READING_MULTIPLE', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4546d57f-b9c6-51c0-9e22-8a9bb7c19de5'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Furniture', E'There are perhaps three ways of looking at furniture: some people see it as purely functional and useful, and don''t bother themselves with aesthetics; others see it as essential to civilized living and concern themselves with design and how the furniture will look in a room - in other words, function combined with aesthetics; and yet others see furniture as a form of art.\nIn the past, designers of furniture usually worked for royalty, the nobility, landowners and rich merchants and so were not constrained by the limits of space, economy, or even practicality that inhibit the contemporary designer. Indeed, function was not the first consideration and interiors did not always have to be practical. In the Renaissance, for example, fine furniture and interiors were designed to show off not only the riches of the owners, but their learning, wisdom and good taste as well. No doubt, this attitude still exists among a number of the wealthy.\nApart from a brief period in the 20th century when furniture designers mistook themselves for artists and sculptors, producing, say, chairs that were nice to look at, but impossible to sit on comfortably, modern designers have, for the most part, come to terms with the functional aspect of furniture. These days, a well-designed interior must be practical and exclude what is unnecessary. Limited space must be used imaginatively, and a sense of space and clarity is needed as a setting for efficient living. Therefore, in the modern home, furniture should fulfil a specific purpose, and need as little care and attention as possible. In addition to this, costs must be kept to a minimum because, these days, there are many luxuries competing for our attention. Function and economy, therefore, are of the utmost importance.\n\nAccording to the text, how does modern furniture design differ from that of the past?', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'Practicality and economy are the most important considerations.\nModern furniture should need little looking after.\nMaking the best use of available space is important.', NULL, NULL),
  ('45505c27-a9e7-59aa-aa28-dbee8fa7d692'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '45505c27-a9e7-59aa-aa28-dbee8fa7d692'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'WFD', NULL, '01998ba4-3724-5c95-af63-47d44ee7da5f'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'There have been too many struggles in the mathematics department.', NULL, NULL),
  ('45510a14-952f-5fe3-a91c-5afc246bdaba'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '45510a14-952f-5fe3-a91c-5afc246bdaba'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'RS', NULL, 'b3307eaf-42f4-545b-818e-d34838b28f53'::uuid, NULL, E'There are many different religions across the world.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('45540e06-9352-5b42-b6ef-d2bafd202773'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '45540e06-9352-5b42-b6ef-d2bafd202773'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'London Taxi Drivers', NULL, '149b77ab-6838-5f7d-97cf-b2b905cd185b'::uuid, NULL, E'But we can really thank the Great Exhibition of 1851 for giving us the world’s premier taxi service, for it was going to this exhibition, and this fabulous exhibition inventions from all around the four corners of the Empire that the visitors were appalled, dismayed and vexed by their journeys to this exhibition because the cabbies of the day, and their horse-drawn carts were absolutely terrible, could not find their way to this exhibition. And, so, a great public outcry, the London Authority sets up Public Carriage Office, which is an organization that still exists. And you can take a short walk to Penton Street up the road. And this Public Carriage office took on the responsibility of licensing all major taxi drivers in London. All taxi drivers from 1851 onwards had to pass what is now known as the London knowledge, was phenomenal knowledge of London. What is the London knowledge? It’s the ability to remember the 25,000 streets, have it all interconnected and all the main arterial roads in and out of London. Cabbies need to know all this plus a thousand points of specific interest cafes, bars, public offices. They need to know them all as part of their training.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('4554a5a2-5f19-5c9d-afdc-87edc8ce4ad5'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4554a5a2-5f19-5c9d-afdc-87edc8ce4ad5'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'RS', NULL, 'f0f04e6d-eed5-5498-8bed-26ac63568930'::uuid, NULL, E'Population growth can be attributed to several factors, including improved education opportunities.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('4556fae7-5c1c-5231-b657-26a6dcbada04'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4556fae7-5c1c-5231-b657-26a6dcbada04'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'RS', NULL, '27b27045-cf78-51a3-9032-03897fcf7e29'::uuid, NULL, E'She has failed to complete her degree in international business.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('456006de-4c20-5270-9d7f-041e9520b5d0'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '456006de-4c20-5270-9d7f-041e9520b5d0'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Air on Mars', E'The rover analysed the air on Mars, and it found that the air contained another of the most basic organic molecules and a key {{1}} of natural gas: methane. One of the characteristics of methane is that it only {{2}} a few hundred years. This means that something, somewhere on Mars, is {{3}} the supply. According to NASA, Mars {{4}} thousands of tons of methane at a time.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.ingredient, 2.survives, 3.replenishing, 4.emits', NULL, NULL),
  ('4568ea29-4f60-5be0-b99f-b76a73532a3d'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4568ea29-4f60-5be0-b99f-b76a73532a3d'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'WFD', NULL, '5e5edd55-4787-5bc8-b4b2-464f09709907'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'True or false questions seem tougher to many than short answers.', NULL, NULL),
  ('4570e0ae-1e59-59f6-920c-07d4ed8db192'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4570e0ae-1e59-59f6-920c-07d4ed8db192'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Energy Conservation', NULL, 'a023a907-2305-5597-9a72-cab295f4c037'::uuid, NULL, E'This phenomenon of conservation is explained by what we call the first law of thermodynamics, sometimes referred to as the law of energy conservation. The law states, energy cannot be created or destroyed. Energy can be described as the ability to do work, where work is the movement of matter when a force is applied to it. A closed system is a system in which no matter or energy is allowed to enter or leave. The first law of thermodynamics tells us that the amount of energy within an ecosystem is constant. It doesn’t change. An open system, on the other hand, allows stuff to come in and go out. Since most systems are not closed, the laws of energy conservation can be rephrased to say that the change in the internal energy of the system is equal to the difference between the amount of energy coming in, minus the amount of energy going out. In other words, the amount of energy in the system can change but only if it comes from another system or goes to another system. At any rate, systems, whether they’re open or closed, do not create or destroy energy. Rather, energy can enter from one system and leave to another.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('4583d3db-dd82-5a05-9a8a-92947e52c5e4'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4583d3db-dd82-5a05-9a8a-92947e52c5e4'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'RS', NULL, '923856a9-34e2-540d-8850-4d29ea93c0c0'::uuid, NULL, E'Smoke played a big role in making air pollution worse.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('458b0340-fc31-51ef-b151-dec1b8642252'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RE_ORDER_PARAGRAPHS', E'RE_ORDER_PARAGRAPHS', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '458b0340-fc31-51ef-b151-dec1b8642252'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Motivation', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, E'3, 2, 4, 1', NULL, NULL),
  ('458c36ee-4e25-536c-aaf6-d0317ed5642a'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'SUMMARIZE_WRITTEN_TEXT', E'SUMMARIZE_WRITTEN_TEXT', E'WRITING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '458c36ee-4e25-536c-aaf6-d0317ed5642a'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Animal Migration', E'The phenomenon of animal migration is one of the most captivating and awe-inspiring aspects of the natural world. It showcases the remarkable journeys undertaken by countless species, often spanning thousands of miles, in search of food, favorable breeding grounds, or milder climates. Migration is not limited to a single type of animal; it occurs among birds, mammals, fish, insects, and even some reptiles. Each species has its unique strategy and adaptations for making these epic journeys. One of the most renowned migrations is that of the monarch butterfly. These fragile insects travel from North America to Mexico, covering thousands of miles. Their journey is a marvel of precision and timing, as multiple generations of butterflies are involved in completing the round-trip migration.\nBirds, too, embark on incredible migrations. The Arctic tern, for example, holds the record for the longest migratory journey of any bird, traveling from the Arctic to the Antarctic and back, covering a staggering 44,000 miles annually. Marine animals like sea turtles and whales migrate vast distances as well. Sea turtles navigate across entire oceans to return to their nesting beaches, while humpback whales undertake migratory journeys between polar feeding grounds and tropical breeding areas. The reasons behind these journeys vary. Some animals migrate to find food, as is the case with many birds that travel to warmer regions in winter to access insect-rich environments. Others migrate for breeding purposes, seeking safe and suitable locations to reproduce and rear their young.\nAnimal migration is not only a testament to the extraordinary adaptability and instincts of these creatures but also a reminder of the interconnectedness of ecosystems across the globe. It is crucial to understand and protect these migration routes and habitats to ensure the survival of countless species.', NULL, NULL, E'Animal migration is one of the most captivating and awe-inspiring aspects of the natural world, which is not limited to a single type of animal, and each species has its unique strategy and adaptations for making these epic journeys; the reasons behind these journeys vary, which means that animal migration is a reminder of the interconnectedness of ecosystems across the globe, so it is crucial to understand and protect these migration routes and habitats.', NULL, 5, 75),
  ('459d85dd-0841-57de-a2db-8319dcd84437'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '459d85dd-0841-57de-a2db-8319dcd84437'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'ASQ', E'What do we call the basic unit of information in computing and digital communications?', 'a69b68e5-aa9e-57c7-9695-b5c1adc82531'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Bit', NULL, NULL),
