  ('4cd4838b-27e9-5fb6-8aca-f8a963a03307'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4cd4838b-27e9-5fb6-8aca-f8a963a03307'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Computer Science', E'What can computer science tell us about what biological systems do and how they do it? Can these chemical information-processing functions be {{1}} in digital computing systems? What are the {{2}} of developments in computer science in understanding the nature of causality? Aaron Sloman, author of Computer Revolution in Philosophy {{3}} into the world of connections between ideas developed in computer science, biology and philosophy, providing new {{4}} into some fundamental questions about the nature of consciousness and free will.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.replicated, 2.implications, 3.delves, 4.insights', NULL, NULL),
  ('4cd84706-e450-57c1-80e9-755accded513'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4cd84706-e450-57c1-80e9-755accded513'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'RS', NULL, 'f1fc1cd8-b7a8-5867-bfc3-3c58ee89662c'::uuid, NULL, E'Our immune systems are killing billions of germs every second.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('4ce887b8-021d-5c72-8aaa-b01338821f02'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4ce887b8-021d-5c72-8aaa-b01338821f02'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'ASQ', E'Name a city in the U.S.', '3d7ea428-eb43-58a5-944d-a498584dd2a3'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'New York / Washington / Boston', NULL, NULL),
  ('4ceeaa8e-ab51-5bbb-b649-2bd0adc4b1bf'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4ceeaa8e-ab51-5bbb-b649-2bd0adc4b1bf'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'ASQ', E'What is the upper part that protects a building?', '6dfb60d0-8fd4-5b8f-b024-e7233b000319'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Roof', NULL, NULL),
  ('4cf63259-3873-5b28-b7a7-9f892058081c'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4cf63259-3873-5b28-b7a7-9f892058081c'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Reinforcement Theory', E'In contrast to some other motivational theories, reinforcement theory ignores the inner state of the individual. Instead it focuses on what happens to an individual when he or she {{1}} some task or action. Reinforcement theorists see behavior as being environmentally controlled. Rather than internal thoughts or desires, the theory is that behaviors are controlled by reinforcers — any consequence that, when immediately following a response, increases the {{2}} that the behavior will be repeated. For example, you decided to work over the weekend to finish a project early for your boss. When your boss finds out about your extra effort, she thanks you and buys you lunch. Assuming your boss’s reactions were {{3}} to you, you will be more likely to do similar deeds in the future. If your boss said or did nothing to acknowledge your extra work, you would be less likely to {{4}} similar behavior in the future.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.performs, 2.probability, 3.favorable, 4.demonstrate', NULL, NULL),
  ('4cf77867-7c8a-56b0-b4f0-33f3a39ae204'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4cf77867-7c8a-56b0-b4f0-33f3a39ae204'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Plagiarism', E'How is plagiarism detected? It is usually easy for lecturers to {{1}} plagiarism within students'' work. The University also actively investigates plagiarism in students'' assessed work through electronic detection software called Turnitin. This software {{2}} students'' work {{3}} text on the Internet, in journal articles and within previously {{4}} work (from LSBU and other institutions) and highlights any matches it {{5}} .', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.identify, 2.compares, 3.against, 4.submitted, 5.finds', NULL, NULL),
  ('4cf77d27-2fe0-508b-a408-086cf9b27ec3'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4cf77d27-2fe0-508b-a408-086cf9b27ec3'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Sound Receptor', NULL, 'f974da5e-fe97-531e-a65e-8c0f5d37e1c5'::uuid, NULL, E'Youʼve got sound receptors in your ear and they are beautiful. Weʼre not going to talk about them at any length, but thereʼs little flappy, these little spiky things going along in your ear and they can translate vibrational energy coming from your ear, hurting your eardrum, being translated into a vibration into the fluid in your ear into a physical motion of these little receptors there into an electrical motion, into an electrical signal that goes into your ear. So, all of that, all of thatʼs pretty impressive stuff. Weʼre not going to talk about the details of it, but I invite some of you who want to learn more about this, particularly MIT students, I think find receptors really quite remarkable kinds of devices.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('4d02479b-a331-5aa2-8d07-ea4f190aadb8'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4d02479b-a331-5aa2-8d07-ea4f190aadb8'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'ASQ', E'What is the name of a building where you can borrow books?', 'c7b65110-b083-50be-ae6c-0dd569f5dbee'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Library', NULL, NULL),
  ('4d077a12-dbc3-58bc-86de-58eb262797a9'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'READ_ALOUD', E'READ_ALOUD', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4d077a12-dbc3-58bc-86de-58eb262797a9'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Consultants', E'Consultants are generally brought in on the important decisions that are vital to the future of the company, to make sure the every angle is considered. They can devote themselves entirely to the question at hand, while executives are normally busy with the actual running of the company. Consultants also offer deep expertise in a particular industry or subject, such as market positioning or restructuring.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('4d0a3b5f-9bde-5b25-8ea5-4a279bcb5014'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'SUMMARIZE_WRITTEN_TEXT', E'SUMMARIZE_WRITTEN_TEXT', E'WRITING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4d0a3b5f-9bde-5b25-8ea5-4a279bcb5014'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Body Electricity', E'Without electricity, you wouldn''t be reading this article right now. And it''s not because your computer wouldn''t work. It''s because your brain wouldn''t work. Everything we do is controlled and enabled by electrical signals running through our bodies. As we learned in intro physics, everything is made up of atoms, and atoms are made up of protons, neutrons and electrons. Protons have a positive charge, neutrons have a neutral charge, and electrons have a negative charge. Note that the atoms themselves can carry a positive or a negative charge, by gaining or losing electrons. The flow of electrons between atoms is what we call electricity. Since our bodies are huge masses of atoms, we can generate electricity. When we talk about the nervous system sending "signals" to the brain, or synapses "firing," or the brain telling our hands to contract around a door handle, what we''re talking about is electricity carrying messages between point A and point B. It''s sort of like the digital cable signal carrying 1s and 0s that deliver "Law & Order” episodes. Except in our bodies, electrons aren''t flowing along a wire; instead, an electrical charge is jumping from one cell to the next until it reaches its destination. Nearly all of our cells have the ability to generate electricity. The starting point is simple: Right now, any cells in your body that aren''t actively sending messages are slightly negatively charged.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 5, 75),
  ('4d0a620d-1ef5-589d-98bc-ed910c4baf71'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'READ_ALOUD', E'READ_ALOUD', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4d0a620d-1ef5-589d-98bc-ed910c4baf71'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Online Treatment', E'Computer- and smartphone-based treatments appear to be effective in reducing symptoms of depression, and while it remains unclear whether they are as effective as face-to-face psychotherapy, they offer a promising alternative to address the growing mental health needs spawned by the COVID-19 pandemic.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('4d111c44-a52c-5735-a317-b250ddae593f'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4d111c44-a52c-5735-a317-b250ddae593f'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Vitamin D', NULL, '3213f0fa-2bfa-536f-8a55-19c684bf9ade'::uuid, NULL, E'How much sun exposure might one need to get their target vitamin D level to that found associated with the lowest total mortality rate? Well, it depends. It depends on our age, how long we''re exposed, the time of day, the time of the year, our latitude, our skin color, our use of sunscreen, and how much of our body we''re exposing. Even in Boston, though, all it takes is 10-12 minutes of mid-day summer sun without sunblock, if you''re a young pale naked Caucasian. But then you''re golden! Actually, you''d be a little pink. Note though, if you are some old white guy prancing around naked on the Commons, you''re not going to make it. As I hope you are beginning to appreciate, it''s not easy to make one-size fits all recommendations for how much sun exposure one might need. And Low vitamin D status despite abundant sun exposure has been found even in the best of circumstances: young half naked skateboarders in Honolulu, mostly Caucasian, averaging 30 hours of sun a week, and 51% didn''t even make it to 30. If they can''t, who can? And these days, even if we''re an albino nudist at the equator, how often might we be getting outside in middle of the day with a desk job? So, if we''re really interested in getting to the vitamin D level associated with the lowest mortality rates, and our lifestyle or latitude won''t allow us the necessary sun exposure, then one needs to take vitamin D supplements: the piddly amount added to soymilk, calf-milk, margarine or mushrooms are simply not enough.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('4d13bbe5-9c3c-54d7-82fc-c8a8c6621e3a'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'READ_ALOUD', E'READ_ALOUD', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4d13bbe5-9c3c-54d7-82fc-c8a8c6621e3a'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Poverty Reduction Strategy', E'Canada''s first-ever Poverty Reduction Strategy is built on the vision that all Canadians should be able to live in dignity. It is also built on the belief that all Canadians deserve to be treated fairly and fully participate in the development of their communities. To be effective, the strategy must have transparent indicators, clear targets and tangible actions.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('4d1b9f5a-b842-5527-91db-d0283e54a995'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RE_ORDER_PARAGRAPHS', E'RE_ORDER_PARAGRAPHS', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4d1b9f5a-b842-5527-91db-d0283e54a995'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Pidgin', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, E'2, 3, 4, 1', NULL, NULL),
  ('4d1c4385-6181-5add-96a8-7c49cff2a302'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'READ_ALOUD', E'READ_ALOUD', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4d1c4385-6181-5add-96a8-7c49cff2a302'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Dream', E'Why do we dream? A product of our brain''s neurophysiology, dreaming is a complex experience that can take on many emotional tones and simulate reality to varying degrees. As a result, there is still no clear answer to this question. A study compared the dreams of two forager communities, in Tanzania and the Democratic Republic of Congo, with those of individuals living in Europe and North America.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('4d242117-753d-57b1-a489-a8cb39f7aa48'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4d242117-753d-57b1-a489-a8cb39f7aa48'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'ASQ', E'How many millimeters are there in a centimeter?', 'c95610b9-5bb8-5532-9e56-bef3baad2527'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Ten', NULL, NULL),
  ('4d2942d7-5d56-5358-b500-d8ec1d33c809'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4d2942d7-5d56-5358-b500-d8ec1d33c809'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Light Pollution', E'The widespread use of artificial lighting in modern society has given {{1}} to light pollution, a prevalent issue in areas with high human population growth. This pollution poses a significant threat to species that {{2}} on natural light and dark cycles. Sea turtles, especially during the hatchling stage, {{3}} ecological challenges due to artificial light, as they make their way from nests to the sea.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.rise, 2.rely, 3.face', NULL, NULL),
  ('4d2a3233-e970-50e6-8e5d-362fb10c73c6'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4d2a3233-e970-50e6-8e5d-362fb10c73c6'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'WFD', NULL, 'bc4bf716-c45b-5e6c-9696-75ad3691a949'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'This course is designed by an innovative faculty from the college.', NULL, NULL),
  ('4d2e461b-9204-5a2a-9a23-0675bfe7441d'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4d2e461b-9204-5a2a-9a23-0675bfe7441d'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Spaced Repetition', NULL, '7cb9fd05-2b89-5a4e-8729-24c6808649fb'::uuid, NULL, E'One evidence-based way to better remember what you’ve learned is through Spaced Repetition, or spacing out your learning and practice of new knowledge or skills. Although this might seem novel, this is hardly a new concept; it was first described in 1885 by a German psychologist named Herman Ebbinghaus. Here’s how it works. Say you plot your retention, or how much you remember of something, vs. time. Now you learn that something on day 0. Without reviewing it, the “forgetting curve” will look like an exponentially decaying curve, which is kind of scary! If you review (or better yet actively retrieve) the material at increasingly spaced intervals after learning it, then the forgetting curve starts to flatten out and you’ll get a lot better longer-term retention. Now, the goal here is to review the material at the right time. It turns out that the best time to revisit information that you are trying to learn is right around the time you would naturally forget it. Since forgetting typically follows this exponential curve, the trick becomes timing your study sessions around it. Practically, this means having more widely spaced intervals between study times for the material that you are more familiar with, and shorter intervals between study sessions for material that you are less familiar with.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('4d395424-60a7-574d-a2fe-150903d314c5'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'SUMMARIZE_WRITTEN_TEXT', E'SUMMARIZE_WRITTEN_TEXT', E'WRITING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4d395424-60a7-574d-a2fe-150903d314c5'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Tax on Meat', E'"A day would come", Percy Shelley predicted in 1813, "when the monopolizing eater of animal flesh would no longer destroy his constitution by eating an acre at a meal." He explained: "The quantity of nutritious vegetable matter consumed in fattening the carcass of an ox would afford 10 times the sustenance if gathered immediately from the bosom of the earth." Two hundred years later, mainstream agronomists and dietitians have caught up with the poet. A growing scientific consensus agrees that feeding cereals and beans to animals is an inefficient and extravagant way to produce human food, that there is a limited amount of grazing land, that the world will be hard-pressed to supply a predicted population of 9 billion people with a diet as rich in meat as the industrialized world currently enjoys, and that it''s not a very healthy diet anyway. On top of this, livestock contribute significantly to global warming, generating 14.5% of all manmade greenhouse gas emissions, according to one much-quoted estimate from the United Nations. Now that the problem has been identified, the challenge is to persuade people in wealthy countries to eat less meat. That might seem a tall order, but governments have successfully persuaded people to quit smoking through a combination of public information, regulation and taxation.', NULL, NULL, E'A growing scientific consensus agrees that feeding cereals and beans to animals is an inefficient and extravagant way to produce human food, and livestock contribute significantly to global warming, since the challenge is to persuade people in wealthy countries to eat less meat, so governments have successfully persuaded people to quit smoking through a combination of public information, regulation and taxation.', NULL, 5, 75),
  ('4d3e3163-dff8-5b96-9ea9-b60c8c648f24'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'READ_ALOUD', E'READ_ALOUD', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4d3e3163-dff8-5b96-9ea9-b60c8c648f24'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Hooked Patients', E'Some of the pain patients may become hooked. Or their meds may find their way to friends or relatives who take them recreationally. Or a prescription opioid user may transition to heroin. Heroin is just another opioid drug, so the brain doesn’t distinguish whether it comes from a pharmacy or from a street drug dealer.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('4d416b92-b396-5ef9-8edb-fcda665050f0'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4d416b92-b396-5ef9-8edb-fcda665050f0'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'WFD', NULL, '03ad4c56-a961-55fa-9d6c-366bb4aefa8d'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'The book provides systematic development of students'' listening and speaking skills.', NULL, NULL),
  ('4d43ed62-5d97-5bae-9de4-848f9bae8145'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'READ_ALOUD', E'READ_ALOUD', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4d43ed62-5d97-5bae-9de4-848f9bae8145'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Home Design', E'One of the major factors influencing future home design will be the probable change in climate, with hotter summers, colder winters, and the possibility of floods. Consequently, houses will be built with better insulation and will also need ways of keeping cool in hot weather, whether that’s air conditioning or more shading of windows.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('4d46b4b0-04f3-5ea3-a02b-a85deddd71ed'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'READ_ALOUD', E'READ_ALOUD', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4d46b4b0-04f3-5ea3-a02b-a85deddd71ed'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Windbreaks', E'Scientists already knew that the output of a single wind turbine could be improved with a windbreak. While windbreaks slow wind speed close to the ground, above the height of the windbreak, wind speeds actually increase as air rushes over the top. But for large wind farms, there’s a drawback.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('4d482c12-3f5c-574f-a3a3-2201b960bb18'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4d482c12-3f5c-574f-a3a3-2201b960bb18'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Potassium and Bone', E'A higher potassium intake has also been {{1}} with increased bone density and bone strength. While researchers are {{2}} as to why, one theory is that potassium helps to neutralize acids that naturally occur when eating animal protein and sodium. Without {{3}} potassium to neutralize these acids, calcium is pulled from the bones to do the job instead, and without enough calcium, bones become weaker.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.associated, 2.unclear, 3.adequate', NULL, NULL),
  ('4d4cfdc7-163e-51ba-9e13-04f58af317d1'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4d4cfdc7-163e-51ba-9e13-04f58af317d1'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'ASQ', E'What do we call the length of a line that goes around a circle or any other curved shape?', 'cc0dc1eb-2bfc-5bef-89c4-614b8b420a39'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Circumference/ Perimeter', NULL, NULL),
  ('4d4d8675-d698-5099-ad1e-a747df8ba6b3'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4d4d8675-d698-5099-ad1e-a747df8ba6b3'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'ASQ', E'What is a standard set of letters that is used to write one or more languages based upon the general principle?', '6218f80b-461e-5e88-9c68-929be546ac73'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Alphabet', NULL, NULL),
  ('4d529de2-ac9a-5184-ad86-67a5b8f0bf51'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'SUMMARIZE_WRITTEN_TEXT', E'SUMMARIZE_WRITTEN_TEXT', E'WRITING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4d529de2-ac9a-5184-ad86-67a5b8f0bf51'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Sleep Time', E'If it seems like you''re not getting enough sleep, you''re not alone. People have evolved to sleep much less than chimps, baboons or any other primates studied so far, a new study finds. Charles Nunn and David Samson are evolutionary anthropologists. They study how humans have evolved to behave the way we do. Nunn works at Duke University in Durham, N.C. Samson works at the University of Toronto Mississauga in Canada. In their new study, the two compared sleep patterns in 30 different species of primates, including humans. Most species slept between nine and 15 hours daily. Humans averaged just seven hours of shut-eye. Based on lifestyle and biological factors, however, people should get 9.55 hours, Nunn and Samson calculate. Most other primates in the study typically sleep as much as the scientists predicted they should. Nunn and Samson shared their findings online February 14 in the American Journal of Physical Anthropology.\nThe researchers argue that two long-standing features of human life may play into our short sleep times. The first stems from when humans'' ancestors descended from the trees to sleep on the ground. At that point, people probably had to spend more time awake to guard against predators. The second may reflect the intense pressure humans face to learn and teach new skills and to make social connections. That has left less time for sleep.\nAs overall sleep declined, rapid-eye movement (REM) sleep took on an outsized role in humans., Nunn and Samson propose. REM sleep is when we dream. And it has been linked to learning and memory.\n"It''s pretty surprising that non-REM sleep time is so low in humans," Nunn says. "But something had to give as we slept less."', NULL, NULL, E'People have evolved to sleep much less than other primates, as two long-standing features of human life may play into our short sleep times, since the first stems from when humans'' ancestors descended from the trees to sleep on the ground, and the second reflects the intense pressure humans face to learn and teach new skills and to make social connections, so as sleep declined, rapid-eye movement sleep took on an outsize role in humans.', NULL, 5, 75),
  ('4d57d861-a642-50a5-a4c3-8dd0f70a1a1b'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4d57d861-a642-50a5-a4c3-8dd0f70a1a1b'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'RS', NULL, 'aed21b37-b940-5fbe-ae92-73b9b321f48a'::uuid, NULL, E'Many students worry about writing essays because they lack experience.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('4d5abb74-af82-54b4-af6c-04372e179160'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4d5abb74-af82-54b4-af6c-04372e179160'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Enhanced Geothermal Systems', E'Enhanced geothermal systems are an exciting development in the world of renewable energy. These systems don''t just use the natural heat found in specific locations, like traditional geothermal energy {{1}} . Instead, they can {{2}} deeper into the Earth to access its heat almost anywhere. This means we could potentially use the Earth''s heat as a reliable and clean source of energy, no {{3}} where we are. The key is new drilling methods that let us go deeper than before. Enhanced geothermal energy is always available, unlike solar or wind energy, which depend on the weather. As we look for ways to use less fossil {{4}} and fight climate change, these systems could be incredibly important. They offer a future where we can get more of our energy in a way that''s good for the planet.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.does, 2.reach, 3.matter, 4.fuel', NULL, NULL),
  ('4d5e712e-6179-5553-879d-b4c3b3bbcbfa'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RE_ORDER_PARAGRAPHS', E'RE_ORDER_PARAGRAPHS', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4d5e712e-6179-5553-879d-b4c3b3bbcbfa'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Ageing Population', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1, 5, 2, 4, 3', NULL, NULL),
  ('4d693944-3cb0-5102-b272-8fb1cf0d7988'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4d693944-3cb0-5102-b272-8fb1cf0d7988'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'RS', NULL, '1f1dbc70-77a0-5aa8-a39b-f1403940b805'::uuid, NULL, E'Electric vehicles signify the future of transportation, promising a more eco-friendly world.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('4d6ba436-5777-55f1-a71f-e02260a56091'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RE_ORDER_PARAGRAPHS', E'RE_ORDER_PARAGRAPHS', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4d6ba436-5777-55f1-a71f-e02260a56091'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Energy Research', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1, 4, 3, 2', NULL, NULL),
  ('4d6daaa7-e54c-57f4-9b19-d797537278c7'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'READ_ALOUD', E'READ_ALOUD', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4d6daaa7-e54c-57f4-9b19-d797537278c7'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Positive Psychology', E'Positive psychology is a relatively new field that emphasizes the promotion of well-being and positive emotions rather than just the treatment of mental illness. Positive psychologists study topics such as happiness, gratitude, and resilience. By promoting positive emotions and behaviors, positive psychology interventions can improve mental health outcomes.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('4d70c9f6-f991-58a0-bd6c-fa095a412f87'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'READ_ALOUD', E'READ_ALOUD', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4d70c9f6-f991-58a0-bd6c-fa095a412f87'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Source of Funding', E'A study found that the research funded by the soft drinks industry had different results from research funded by other sources and went on to suggest that they may have been biased by the research itself. The whole point of the scientific methods is to ensure the research results are not influenced by the source of funding.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('4d71bfbd-aefa-5e78-9e67-cf5404d6bc47'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'READ_ALOUD', E'READ_ALOUD', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '4d71bfbd-aefa-5e78-9e67-cf5404d6bc47'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Climate Change', E'Largely driven by human activity, climate change has been messing with the traditional concept of four seasons for a while now. Scientists have discovered that as the planet warms up, the tropics have been expanding by 0.1 to 0.2 degrees of latitude every decade, so that places that once had four seasons are shifting to having just two.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
