  ('5bcfd34d-1136-5d48-a487-11a99d0aa0c3'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RE_ORDER_PARAGRAPHS', E'RE_ORDER_PARAGRAPHS', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5bcfd34d-1136-5d48-a487-11a99d0aa0c3'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Great Wall of China', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1, 3, 2, 4', NULL, NULL),
  ('5bd1567b-7964-53ab-b3db-6b177a658495'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5bd1567b-7964-53ab-b3db-6b177a658495'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'RS', NULL, 'b6862ac8-522f-5d77-a50a-b6a8c526e0d2'::uuid, NULL, E'Social robots can express various emotions through facial expressions effectively.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('5bd1d43c-fc3f-5689-aff4-a6f41412ca02'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5bd1d43c-fc3f-5689-aff4-a6f41412ca02'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'ASQ', E'To improve their health and fitness, most people either try to improve their diet or...?', 'd1e79f21-6231-5738-9b08-5a016277e7ad'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Do more physical exercise', NULL, NULL),
  ('5bd2447f-af0e-587c-8c24-b349313feb78'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5bd2447f-af0e-587c-8c24-b349313feb78'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Education and Well-being', E'Education and well-being have often been {{1}} . The idea that education can promote individual well-being indirectly, by {{2}} earnings and promoting {{3}} mobility, is an old one; so are notions of education helping to promote the good society by {{4}} to economic growth and equality of opportunity.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.associated, 2.improving, 3.social, 4.contributing', NULL, NULL),
  ('5bdf7f44-a343-5357-8e4d-7d935e87a160'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5bdf7f44-a343-5357-8e4d-7d935e87a160'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Choking Under Pressure', NULL, 'e71230dd-165f-50cc-9f6b-20993e423aa6'::uuid, NULL, E'Most people intuitively blame it on their nerves, but why does being nervous undermine expert performance? There are two sets of theories, which both say that primarily choking under pressure boils down to focus. First, there are the distraction theories. These suggest that performance suffers when the mind is preoccupied with worries, doubts, or fears, instead of focusing its attention on performing the task at hand. When relevant and irrelevant thoughts compete for the same attention, something has to give. The brain can only process so much information at once. Tasks that challenge working memory, the mental scratch pad we use to temporarily store phone numbers and grocery lists, are especially vulnerable to pressure. In a 2004 study, a group of university students were asked to perform math problems, some easy, others more complex and memory-intensive. Half the students completed both problem types with nothing at stake, while the others completed them when calm and under pressure. While everyone did well on the easy problems, those who were stressed performed worse on the more difficult, memory-intensive tasks. Explicit monitoring theories make up the second group of explanations for choking under pressure. They’re concerned with how pressure can cause people to overanalyze the task at hand. Here, the logic goes that once a skill becomes automatic, thinking about its precise mechanics interferes with your ability to do it. Tasks we do unconsciously seem to be most vulnerable to this kind of choking.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('5be30c3e-50f9-5209-9d3b-2432f1cdb32b'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5be30c3e-50f9-5209-9d3b-2432f1cdb32b'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'WFD', NULL, '07d69f94-f710-50c2-a521-977c8520439b'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Today a number of students have volunteer jobs.', NULL, NULL),
  ('5be5d24a-e196-538c-9bb8-fdc05dcf6610'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5be5d24a-e196-538c-9bb8-fdc05dcf6610'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'RS', NULL, 'a278bcb3-f141-5f64-b05e-2948c21be76c'::uuid, NULL, E'If you knew how to get there in time, why didn''t you tell him?', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('5bf21b3e-3255-5821-a7c7-52c6a7448ae6'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'READ_ALOUD', E'READ_ALOUD', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5bf21b3e-3255-5821-a7c7-52c6a7448ae6'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Electricity Discoveries', E'The Royal Institution is an organization that has been around for 209 years. Many of the people that have worked here have been scientists themselves, including Michael Faraday. He made the discoveries that made generating and using electricity much easier, making it possible for us all to switch on lights, cook our dinner, play games consoles much, much more.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('5bf49e52-82cc-5916-a37b-250dea481c0a'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5bf49e52-82cc-5916-a37b-250dea481c0a'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Open Door Policy', E'An "Open Door" policy ensures equal commercial and industrial rights for the nationals of all countries within a certain territory. As a {{1}} policy, it was first advanced by the United States, but it was {{2}} in the typical most-favored-nation clause of the treaties concluded with China after the Opium War (1839-42). Although the Open Door is generally {{3}} with China, it also received recognition at the Berlin Conference of 1885, which declared that no power could {{4}} preferential duties in the Congo basin.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.specific, 2.rooted, 3.associated, 4.levy', NULL, NULL),
  ('5bf5f4f2-edcf-56fc-8f87-f1b2ec39882f'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'HIGHLIGHT_CORRECT_SUMMARY', E'HIGHLIGHT_CORRECT_SUMMARY', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5bf5f4f2-edcf-56fc-8f87-f1b2ec39882f'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Petersburg', E'Few cities had a worse location than st. Petersburg in russia. In seventeen o three, russian tsar peter the first began building a great european city for himself, st. Petersburg. There were three main problems with the city. Firstly, he decided to construct his beautiful capital at the mouth of a river. The land around the site was under water most of the time and was either frozen or flooded. Over a hundred thousand construction workers died working in such difficult conditions. The second problem was the availability of construction workers. Few people lived in that area. And the high death rate meant that peter needed to bring in a constant supply of replacement workers from other parts of russia using the military service system. He ordered forty thousand men each year to go and work on his capital. The men were expected to provide their own tools and food for the journey, often with soldiers watching over them, and they were chained together to prevent them from running away. Illness was everywhere, and punishments went from whipping to death. The final problem was supplying the materials themselves. Whole forests were cleared to provide wood hills flattened and leagues filled in. Stone became so hard to find that peter band, anyone else in russia from using it? It was a truly impossible city to build and made him unpopular.\n\nClick on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', '300fcf92-413d-59dc-be1b-431271576c54'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'There were three challenges with the construction of St Petersburg: the unsuitability of the wet land in the location he chose, the difficulty of transporting builders to the remote location, and the poor conditions they were working in and the lack of raw materials for the construction process.', NULL, NULL),
  ('5bfed45a-1185-50e0-a613-e7c3b82db8e3'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'SUMMARIZE_WRITTEN_TEXT', E'SUMMARIZE_WRITTEN_TEXT', E'WRITING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5bfed45a-1185-50e0-a613-e7c3b82db8e3'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Wright Brothers (B)', E'After the 1905 flying season, the Wrights contacted the United States War Department, as well as governments and individuals in England, France, Germany, and Russia, offering to sell a flying machine. They were turned down time and time again -- government bureaucrats thought they were crackpots; others thought that if two bicycle mechanics could build a successful airplane, they could do it themselves. But the Wright persisted, and in late 1907, the U.S. Army Signal Corps asked for an aircraft. Just a few months later, in early 1908, a French syndicate of businessmen agreed to purchase another.\nBoth the U.S. Army and the French asked for an airplane capable of carrying a passenger. The Wright brothers hastily adapted their 1905 Flyer with two seats and a more powerful engine. They tested these modifications in secret, back at Kitty Hawk, North Carolina for the first time in several years. Then the brothers parted temporarily -- Wilbur to France and Orville to Virginia.\nIn 1908 and 1909, Wilbur demonstrated Wright aircraft in Europe, and Orville flew in Fort Meyer, Virginia. The flights went well until Orville lost a propeller and crashed, breaking his leg and killing his passenger Lt. Thomas Selfridge. While Orville recuperated, Wilbur kept flying in France, breaking record after record. Orville and his sister Kate eventually joined Wilbur in France, and the three returned home to Dayton to an elaborate homecoming celebration. Together, Orville and Wilbur returned to Fort Meyer with a new Military Flyer and completed the U.S. Army trials. A few months later, Wilbur flew before over a million spectators in New York Harbor — his first public flight in his native land. All of these flights stunned and captivated the world. The Wright Brothers became the first great celebrities of the twentieth century.', NULL, NULL, E'After the 1905 flying season, the Wrights contacted the United States War Department, offering to sell a flying machine, and they were turned down time and time again, but the Wright persisted, and in late 1907, the U.S. Army Signal Corps asked for an aircraft, after which all of these flights stunned and captivated the world, so the Wright Brothers became the first great celebrities of the twentieth century.', NULL, 5, 75),
  ('5bffb3ca-6dc1-55a7-8ee6-78d3243961ce'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5bffb3ca-6dc1-55a7-8ee6-78d3243961ce'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'RS', NULL, 'e004967c-2fdf-5d4c-b8ce-76b9c2d0fcc8'::uuid, NULL, E'I can give you a hand if you need help.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('5c027afa-27ef-5561-9ac1-43aeac2e2ee1'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'SUMMARIZE_WRITTEN_TEXT', E'SUMMARIZE_WRITTEN_TEXT', E'WRITING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5c027afa-27ef-5561-9ac1-43aeac2e2ee1'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Forensic Anthropology', E'There are a number of applications of anthropology to the forensic sciences. A large part of physical anthropology deals with skeletal biology, which includes bone and bone system structures and their relationships to characteristics such as gender, age, race, socioeconomic status, and so forth. That knowledge can be applied to the examination of characteristics of skeletal remains that are part of a crime scene. In such cases, the goal of the analysis may be to determine the identity of the deceased person and, perhaps, the cause of death. To those ends, forensic anthropologists make use of a number of unique techniques. Two major types of human-remains evidence confront the forensic anthropologist. First is the single bone or bone fragment or small group of bones. When that is the only type of evidence present, the forensic anthropologist seeks to determine if the bone is human and, if not, what type of animal the bone belongs to. If the sample is human bone, then the anthropologist will determine the part of the body from which it came. The second major type of forensic anthropological evidence is the complete skeleton. From that evidence, the accomplished forensic anthropologist may be able to determine gender, race, approximate age, stature, and approximate socioeconomic status. If there is damage to some of the bones, the anthropologist may be able to determine what type of trauma caused it. If the skull is present, it may be possible to prepare an approximate face on the skull using skull superimposition. Investigators may then publish a picture of the face to see if it evokes a response from a relative of a missing person.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 5, 75),
  ('5c05c2aa-5ee8-57e1-bd13-8b77214458b2'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5c05c2aa-5ee8-57e1-bd13-8b77214458b2'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'WFD', NULL, 'a52c5fc2-9bc2-5404-ac53-19e67079adca'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Our school works with others around the world to swap students.', NULL, NULL),
  ('5c14b327-1154-5795-b550-f3e3ddf7ea46'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'READ_ALOUD', E'READ_ALOUD', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5c14b327-1154-5795-b550-f3e3ddf7ea46'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Biological Processes', E'For centuries, the study of biological processes has inspired fundamental mathematical developments, while mathematical analyses have been instrumental in developing a mechanistic understanding of biological observations. This fruitful cross-fertilization has gained momentum over recent decades, thanks in part to extraordinary technological advancements in the biological sciences, as well as the rising availability of large-scale computational resources.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('5c1d8cc1-c202-555d-a4bd-98ddd37617a2'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5c1d8cc1-c202-555d-a4bd-98ddd37617a2'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'City of Rome', NULL, '040192e2-9848-5d6b-88e8-9068e41e1c6e'::uuid, NULL, E'But you can see from the relatively crooked and narrow streets of the city of Rome as they look from above today, You can see that again, the city grew in a fairly ad hoc way, as I mentioned. It wasn''t planned all at once. It just grew up over time, beginning in the eighth century B.C..Now this is interesting. Because what we know about the Romans is when they were left to their own devices and they could build the city from scratch, they didn''t let it grow in an ad hoc way. They, they structured it in a, in a very care-, very methodical way. That was basically based on military strategy, military planning. The Romans they couldn''t have conquered the world without obviously having a masterful military enterprise. And they everywhere they went on their various campaigns, their various military campaigns .They would build, build camps and those camps were always laid out in a very geometric plan along a grid, usually square or rectangular.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('5c235fbb-503e-5bce-ae9d-d5cc24e8767a'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5c235fbb-503e-5bce-ae9d-d5cc24e8767a'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'ASQ', E'In a race, what is the color of a medal that the person winning the first prize gets?', '714d1adf-f2c9-5402-99e8-ee7eb5cfc7b4'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Gold / golden', NULL, NULL),
  ('5c23f75d-d8ed-5cd8-931e-48f9b8b24b91'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5c23f75d-d8ed-5cd8-931e-48f9b8b24b91'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Questionnaire Types', NULL, '07ce414e-e77d-572c-a6c0-ef948f9a4f1e'::uuid, NULL, E'What live decided to provide is the steps that I take when analyzing my own questionnaires. However, before I begin, it would be useful to remind you of a few terms we use when talking about questionnaires. Questions can be divided into three types. This is sometimes called level measurement. Firstly, we have category type questions, which are also known as nominal questions. These are when participants select from a list of categories for their response, such as male or female or they may include ethnic origin. Secondly, we have ordinal type questions. These are similar to category questions. But instead of the categories being independent, there is some sort of order between them. If we ask people to indicate their age in categories. This is an ordinal type question. Thirdly, we have continuous questions. These are any questions that can be answered by a number. It could be an open-ended question asking participants to tell you how many times they attended lectures or how often they used a VLE. Or it could involve asking them to rate the importance of intensity of some experience', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('5c2d9222-e35f-5341-be3d-6abd5eddb71b'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5c2d9222-e35f-5341-be3d-6abd5eddb71b'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'RS', NULL, '1d93d9d3-6754-5629-95f9-fd1738ff4375'::uuid, NULL, E'It''s a great privilege to welcome our guest speaker to our college.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('5c3872a3-6fbf-5680-ad8b-aa3dc9a43f47'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5c3872a3-6fbf-5680-ad8b-aa3dc9a43f47'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Manufacturers', NULL, '41876257-b507-5615-abda-48b6b04ec283'::uuid, NULL, E'Current studies show that what goes on labels is an important consideration for manufacturers, since more than seventy percent of shoppers read food labels when considering whether to buy a product. A recent controversy as to whether labels on prepared foods should educate or merely inform the consumer is over, and a consumer group got its way. The group had maintained that product labels should do more than simply list how many grams of nutrients a food contains. Their contention was that labels should also list the percentage of a day''s total nutrients that the product will supply to the consumer, because this information is essential in planning a healthy diet. A government agency disagreed strongly, favoring a label that merely informs the consumer, in other words, a label that only lists the contents of the products. The agency maintained that consumers could decide for themselves if the food is nutritious and is meeting their daily needs. The consumer group, in supporting its case, had cited a survey in which shoppers were shown a food label, and were then asked if they would need more or less of a certain nutrient after eating a serving of this product. The shoppers weren''t able to answer the questions easily when they were not given a specific percentage. This study, and others helped get the new regulation passed, and now food products must have the more detailed labels.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('5c42e8f7-12d0-5d5d-9d0e-39470f3a6246'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5c42e8f7-12d0-5d5d-9d0e-39470f3a6246'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Animal Dialect', E'We humans are social {{1}} —we stick together. Family traditionally came first. Then more distant relatives. Then larger groups of unrelated individuals, connected by culture. But it turns out the same could be said for sperm whales. "So usually you find the female, and their mom and the grandmas and their aunts, and they all stay together for many years."\nMaurício Cantor, a biologist at Dalhousie University in Halifax, Nova Scotia. He says those closely related family groups then seek out other families with similar behavior: "They tend to hang out or stay together with those who produce the same kinds of sounds." Meaning whale cliques are based on {{2}} similar clicks.\nAnd similarly clicking whales don''t just hang out together, he says. They also emulate each other''s songs, or codas. Meaning clans of whales evolve their own {{3}} —their own form of culture. And these dialects are key. Cantor and colleagues built a computer simulation of generation after generation of virtual whales. And they found that no other factor—like genetics or mother-daughter teaching—could explain the emergence of the clans and dialects in real sperm whale society. The study appears in the journal Nature Communications.\n"I''m not trying to say that the types of culture the whale has are the same as human culture. Obviously human culture is much more diverse and complex and cumulative and symbolic. But it''s very fascinating just to see that they can have some type of {{4}} , they can have their own type of culture." And maybe a better understanding of that whale culture, he says, might persuade a few human cultures to be a bit more conservation-minded, when it comes to whales.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.creatures, 2.producing, 3.dialects, 4.similarities', NULL, NULL),
  ('5c4b4299-06bf-5717-b2bf-34f78ddf4f57'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'READ_ALOUD', E'READ_ALOUD', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5c4b4299-06bf-5717-b2bf-34f78ddf4f57'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Globalization', E'Globalization has transformed the world economy, facilitating the flow of goods, services, and capital across borders. It has led to increased competition and efficiency, as businesses seek to expand their markets and reduce costs. However, globalization has also contributed to economic inequality, as some countries and individuals have benefited more than others.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('5c4c2446-9dd5-5519-a070-205ca00b1dd4'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'SUMMARIZE_WRITTEN_TEXT', E'SUMMARIZE_WRITTEN_TEXT', E'WRITING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5c4c2446-9dd5-5519-a070-205ca00b1dd4'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Asda', E'Asda has become the first food retailer in the country to measure how much customers can save by cutting back on food waste, thanks to a Knowledge Transfer Partnership (KTP) with the University of Leeds. The idea behind the KTP was for the University, using Asda’s customer insight data, to apply its research to identify, investigate and implement ways of helping customers to reduce their food waste. This was one of the first times that a major retailer had tried to deliver large-scale sustainability changes, with the two year project seen as a way for Asda to position themselves as true innovators in this area. The campaign focused on providing customers with advice on everything from food storage and labelling, to creative recipes for leftovers. Meanwhile, in-store events encouraged customers to make changes in their own. They will make changes to how they deal with food waste in their own homes, leading to an average saving of 57 pounds per customer, as well as a reduction in waste. A key aspect of a KTP is that an associate is employed by the University to work in the firm and help deliver the desired outcomes of the KTP. As a part of the collaboration with Asda, Laura Babbs was given the task of driving forward the sustainability changes in the retailer. As a result of the success of her work, Laura eventually became a permanent member of the team at Asda.', NULL, NULL, E'Asda has become the first food retailer in the country to measure how much customers can save by cutting back on food waste, with a campaign focusing on providing customers with advice on everything from food storage and labelling, to creative recipes for leftovers, and with in-store events encouraging customers to make changes in their own, and an associate is employed by the University to work in the firm.', NULL, 5, 75),
  ('5c4ed5b0-fc4d-5c1b-b91f-cfe0ef85fc70'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5c4ed5b0-fc4d-5c1b-b91f-cfe0ef85fc70'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'WFD', NULL, '2346b6ad-89e6-5037-b5f6-43c0eeefe0db'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'You must inform your professor if you are absent from the class.', NULL, NULL),
  ('5c52af62-ae23-5d1d-aa52-f518bb6ef481'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5c52af62-ae23-5d1d-aa52-f518bb6ef481'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'RS', NULL, '7419bcc8-9a23-5a37-983b-37d267102e46'::uuid, NULL, E'She has been collecting material for her latest novel for more than a month.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('5c5ec1b7-81aa-5938-ac34-9c5b91f6b48c'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RE_ORDER_PARAGRAPHS', E'RE_ORDER_PARAGRAPHS', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5c5ec1b7-81aa-5938-ac34-9c5b91f6b48c'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Audrey Hepburn', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, E'2, 5, 4, 1, 3', NULL, NULL),
  ('5c5f162b-cd68-5074-a858-c5b94de78922'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5c5f162b-cd68-5074-a858-c5b94de78922'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'WFD', NULL, '871b8e81-9373-5bd5-99ce-89c6451f9310'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Steam power was the driving force of Europe''s Industrial Revolution.', NULL, NULL),
  ('5c60421e-fce9-50ff-8348-b9ddd712480b'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'READ_ALOUD', E'READ_ALOUD', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5c60421e-fce9-50ff-8348-b9ddd712480b'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Founding Fathers', E'The founding fathers established constitutional protections for the press because they understood that leaving the watchdog function to partisan politicians wouldn’t necessarily serve the public interest; both sides have too many incentives to preserve the status quo and ignore problems that elude quick fixes.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('5c611a18-2ec8-5117-95b4-6f24cafeb7c9'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5c611a18-2ec8-5117-95b4-6f24cafeb7c9'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Capsaicin', E'Here''s a possible blood {{1}} remedy. But it''s only for those who can stand the heat. It''s capsaicin, the "active ingredient" in peppers like habaneros that should probably be sold by prescription only. While lips burn and eyes water, blood vessels {{2}} relax, thanks to increased {{3}} of the signaling molecule nitric oxide. In rodents, anyway.\nFor seven months, {{4}} in China fed a steady diet of capsaicin to rats bred to be hypertensive. Long-term consumption of the chemical {{5}} lowered the rats'' blood pressure. The results appear in the August issue of the journal Cell Metabolism.\nPrevious research found mixed results with capsaicin, but those studies only looked at {{6}} effects. Human trials are needed, but there''s already a clue. Some 20 percent of people in northeastern China have high blood pressure. But the southwest—where hot peppers are a dietary staple—has a much lower incidence, half in some places.\nHuman studies could also confirm whether the habanero, as legend has it, can cause hearing loss. Allegedly so that diners don''t have to listen to their own screams.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.pressure, 2.actually, 3.production, 4.researchers, 5.substantially, 6.short-term', NULL, NULL),
  ('5c65df45-ef5a-5d95-be5d-498abecf882c'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5c65df45-ef5a-5d95-be5d-498abecf882c'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'WFD', NULL, '9ca64c66-60b3-5409-860a-befd9bb6eddb'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Astrology is not an ideal science in poor rural areas due to limited evidence.', NULL, NULL),
  ('5c661d9d-11c6-5259-9614-c37d5bb11f23'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_ESSAY', E'WRITE_ESSAY', E'WRITING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5c661d9d-11c6-5259-9614-c37d5bb11f23'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'High-level Jobs', E'Most high-level jobs are done by men. Should governments reserve a certain percentage of these jobs for women?', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 200, 300),
  ('5c733f5f-8496-516d-bb1d-59fda3055a26'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5c733f5f-8496-516d-bb1d-59fda3055a26'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Ironbridge Gorge', E'The Ironbridge Gorge World Heritage property covers an area of 5.5 km2 (550ha) and is located in Telford, Shropshire, approximately 50km north-west of Birmingham. The Industrial Revolution {{1}} in the Ironbridge Gorge before spreading across the world, bringing with it some of the most far-reaching changes in human history. The site {{2}} the steep-sided, mineral-rich Severn Valley from a point immediately west of Ironbridge downstream to Coalport, together with two smaller river valleys extending northwards to Coalbrookdale and Madeley. The Ironbridge Gorge {{3}} into the origins of the Industrial Revolution and also contains extensive remains of that period when the area was the focus of international attention from artists, engineers, and writers. The site contains substantial remains of mines, foundries, factories, workshops, warehouses, ironmasters'' and workers'' housing, public buildings, infrastructure, and transport systems, together with {{4}} of the Severn Gorge. In addition, there also remain {{5}} of artifacts and archives relating to the individuals, processes, and products that made the area so important.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.had its 18th century roots, 2.incorporates a 5km length of, 3.offers a powerful insight, 4.traditional landscape and forests, 5.extensive collections', NULL, NULL),
  ('5c78e8eb-1035-590b-8539-73aafa045ef2'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5c78e8eb-1035-590b-8539-73aafa045ef2'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Coastal Pollution', E'Coastal pollution refers to the introduction of substances or any form of energy in the coastal environment, which results or is likely to result in {{1}} effects in the ecosystem due to changes in its physical, chemical, and biological {{2}} . Ecological consequences of coastal pollution include {{3}} of habitats and biodiversity, and alteration of environmental functions and processes, resulting in a greater {{4}} to disturbances and in the decline of ecosystem services.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.adverse, 2.characteristics, 3.degradation, 4.susceptibility', NULL, NULL),
  ('5c7994ab-cf2a-5a5b-9531-2d402795fda5'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'SUMMARIZE_WRITTEN_TEXT', E'SUMMARIZE_WRITTEN_TEXT', E'WRITING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5c7994ab-cf2a-5a5b-9531-2d402795fda5'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Archaeological Dig', E'The process of researching and securing a dig site can take years. Digging is the field work of archaeology. On occasion, archaeologists might need to move earth with bulldozers and backhoes. Usually, however, archaeologists use tools such as brushes, hand shovels, and even toothbrushes to scrape away the earth around artifacts. The most common tool that archaeologists use to dig is a flat trowel. A trowel is a hand-held shovel used for smoothing as well as digging. Archaeologists use trowels to slowly scrape away soil. For very small or delicate remains, archaeologists might also dig with dental picks, spoons, or very fine blades. Often, they will sift dirt through a fine mesh screen. Tiny remains, such as beads, can often be found this way.\nArchaeologists take lots of notes and photographs along each step of the process. Sometimes they include audio and video recordings. Global positioning system (GPS) units and data from geographic information systems (GIS) help them map the location of various features with a high level of precision. When archaeologists find remains, they are often broken or damaged after hundreds or even thousands of years underground. Sunlight, rain, soil, animals, bacteria, and other natural processes can cause artifacts to erode, rust, rot, break, and warp.\nSometimes, however, natural processes can help preserve materials. For example, sediments from floods or volcanic eruptions can encase materials and preserve them. In one case, the chill of an Alpine glacier preserved the body of a man for more than 5,300 years! The discoverer of the so-called ‘Iceman,’ found in the Alps between Switzerland and Italy, thought he was a recent victim of murder, or one of the glacier’s crevasses. Forensic archaeologists studying his body were surprised to learn that he was a murder victim — the crime just took place more than 5,000 years ago.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 5, 75),
  ('5c7b4217-3b1d-558f-87b3-9ba0d79340d9'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5c7b4217-3b1d-558f-87b3-9ba0d79340d9'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Power of Simple Word', NULL, '84336ac3-2ea1-5d92-9f3c-ed15b4cd9978'::uuid, NULL, E'We all know the scene: Dorothy closes her eyes, and repeats the Good Witch''s mantra, "No coordinates exist like one''s domicile, no coordinates exist like one''s domicile, no coordinates exist like one''s domicile." Only Dorothy doesn''t say that. She says five one-syllable words, "There''s no place like home." Each a word you probably learned in your first year of speaking, each perfectly concise. It''s not that L. Frank Baum didn''t have a thesaurus. It''s that in most cases $10 words fail. When it comes to words, bigger isn''t always better. Ten-dollar words are rendered worthless if they’re not understood. That’s not to say every piece of literature should be written at a fourth-grade reading level, but it is important to know your audience. If you''re a novelist, your audience is probably expecting 300 pages of vivid descriptors. At the very least, they''re expecting you won’t use the same 50 words to fill those pages. But most of us don’t have the luxury of a captive audience. We''re competing against a whole world of distractions and we''re fighting for space in an attention span that continues to shrink across generations. So get to the point already.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('5c84c974-e9b6-54ec-bd0d-b3e4b7932c51'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5c84c974-e9b6-54ec-bd0d-b3e4b7932c51'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'RS', NULL, 'c49c1bf9-99ee-51f1-8986-c9ede28fe056'::uuid, NULL, E'The program is for technical and scientific students.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('5c86bd0b-1896-5588-9dc6-e9cf645776a3'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5c86bd0b-1896-5588-9dc6-e9cf645776a3'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Durkheim', E'Durkheim found humanistic studies uninteresting, {{1}} from psychology and philosophy to ethics and eventually, sociology. He graduated with a degree in philosophy in 1882. Durkheim''s views {{2}} him a major academic appointment in Paris, so from 1882 to 1887 he taught philosophy at several provincial schools. In 1885 he {{3}} Germany, where he studied sociology for two years. Durkheim''s period in Germany {{4}} the publication of numerous articles on German social science and philosophy, which {{5}} in France, earning him a teaching appointment at the University of Bordeaux in 1887.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.turning his attention, 2.could not get, 3.left for, 4.resulted in, 5.gained recognition', NULL, NULL),
  ('5c877a25-e936-5a59-a7cc-11321acacc52'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5c877a25-e936-5a59-a7cc-11321acacc52'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Civilization and Art', NULL, 'dda70d71-f102-52d0-aa70-477bba10b882'::uuid, NULL, E'I have said before that you can''t have a civilization that doesn''t have art. When we think about the great civilizations historically, all of them had great production of culture and art, because a society has to be able to observe itself. And the sophistication of the great civilizations were their ability to look at themselves and what allows a society to do that. Are the producers of Art and Culture mirror back to the core of the society? Exactly what is being produced at that moment? How people are thinking of themselves and how individuals are relating to the social structure at that time? Art is the vehicle through which we understand that. Were you to take away art? What would be that mirror?How would we see what we are about? How would we understand what was going on in Paris at the time of the impressionists when people were learning to see in a completely different way. Pre cinematograph appear all of these things are just emerging and here are people looking at the world in a very different way which was considered so radical at the time.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('5c8ecb4a-2b3f-5a43-8a3d-3d27cb91e1e7'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'READ_ALOUD', E'READ_ALOUD', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5c8ecb4a-2b3f-5a43-8a3d-3d27cb91e1e7'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Microscope', E'The principal way a microbiologist studies microorganisms is by observing them through a microscope. A microscope is a device that enlarges objects using a process called magnification. The simplest form of a microscope is a magnifying glass consisting of a single lens. Some glasses are slightly bent at the bottom, causing a magnifying effect if held at a certain height over an object.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('5c941f49-c854-5dca-9376-eb8cd7e29691'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'SUMMARIZE_WRITTEN_TEXT', E'SUMMARIZE_WRITTEN_TEXT', E'WRITING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5c941f49-c854-5dca-9376-eb8cd7e29691'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Significance of History', E'History, as a discipline, is not merely about recounting past events; it is a vital tool for understanding our present and shaping our future. The study of history provides us with insights into human behavior, societal trends, and the consequences of past actions.\nOne of the most crucial aspects of history is its role in fostering critical thinking. Historians analyze primary and secondary sources, question biases, and construct narratives based on evidence. This analytical approach encourages individuals to think critically, evaluate information, and make informed decisions in various aspects of life.\nFurthermore, history offers valuable lessons. By examining the successes and failures of past societies, we can learn from their experiences. For example, studying the consequences of wars, conflicts, and diplomatic efforts helps us understand the importance of diplomacy, negotiation, and conflict resolution in today''s globalized world.\nHistory also plays a significant role in shaping national and cultural identities. It helps communities connect with their roots, understand their heritage, and preserve their traditions. It fosters a sense of belonging and pride, contributing to social cohesion.\nMoreover, history is a tool for preventing the repetition of past mistakes. The knowledge of historical injustices, such as genocide, discrimination, and human rights abuses, serves as a reminder of the need to protect human dignity and promote equality.\nIn conclusion, history is not confined to the past; it is a living discipline that informs our present and guides our future. Through critical thinking, valuable lessons, cultural identity, and the prevention of historical mistakes, history continues to shape societies, nations, and individuals, making it an indispensable field of study.', NULL, NULL, E'The study of history serves as a vital tool for fostering critical thinking, offering valuable lessons, shaping cultural identities, and preventing the repetition of past mistakes, making it an essential discipline that informs our present and guides our future by providing insights into human behavior, societal trends, and the consequences of past actions.', NULL, 5, 75),
