  ('5e51e4f3-72db-57da-bfe0-6d513081146d'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_ESSAY', E'WRITE_ESSAY', E'WRITING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5e51e4f3-72db-57da-bfe0-6d513081146d'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Academic Subject', E'Students can obtain information about academic subjects from online sources, printed books and articles, and discussions with their teachers and peers. Which one is the most reliable source?', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 200, 300),
  ('5e57b44c-bc37-535c-ae8a-9252e800336c'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5e57b44c-bc37-535c-ae8a-9252e800336c'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'ASQ', E'What do we call the sport of racing in water by moving the arms and legs?', 'c53d9e27-f271-54a9-9f3c-6b99a06f4bb3'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Swimming', NULL, NULL),
  ('5e5b8a72-f89b-5485-8d5c-0b0b0a80b1a2'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5e5b8a72-f89b-5485-8d5c-0b0b0a80b1a2'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'ASQ', E'What do we call the height above the sea level?', '7a56a859-7c7c-5dc2-b9a0-d03e6324f009'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Altitude', NULL, NULL),
  ('5e5d26b6-0739-5356-a927-fe20c76899f5'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5e5d26b6-0739-5356-a927-fe20c76899f5'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'UK Culture-Manners', NULL, '7e010c6b-f0d1-57e4-90e9-e868ccc59d27'::uuid, NULL, E'I think one of the things that''s confusing for people is when they come here is there appear to be hundreds and hundreds of rules, hundreds of things you should and shouldn''t do. And the truth of it is that most of them are about class. And lots of them are trip-wires actually for people who don''t know them.\nSo what I tried to do in my book was take it back to some sort of first principle and say look - there are anthropologica reasons why we have certain kinds of manners. So I''ll give you a very good example, in Britain there are sort of two ways of holding a knife, very broadly. And broadly speaking the middle-classes hold it with the index finger on the top, gripped in the hand. And working-class people hold it like a pen. Entirely a class distinction and people mercilessly exploit it if they want to. The truth of it is, the one way not to hold a knife at the table, is clasped in your fist, raised as if to kill your guest. And what does that tell us about eating? Well, what it tells us about eating is two things: it is never confuse your guests with either the food or the enemy. Don''t eat them and don''t kill them!That''s about how you should hold your knife, because actually manners are really about the reduction of violence. There''s a lot in there about reducing violence. So that''s just an illustration of what one tries to do so actually when you look at real table manners they''re about people feeling comfortable with each other, sharing food around a table. Very important human thing.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('5e5dbdc0-97dd-5e93-a39e-1a5db0c71afd'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5e5dbdc0-97dd-5e93-a39e-1a5db0c71afd'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Physical Activity', E'Participating regularly in physical activity has been shown to benefit an individual''s health and {{1}} . Regular {{2}} activity is important in reducing the risk of {{3}} diseases, such as heart disease and stroke, obesity, diabetes and some forms of cancer. The National Physical Activity Guidelines for Adults {{4}} at least 30 minutes of moderate-intensity physical activity, {{5}} every day of the week, to {{6}} health benefits.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.wellbeing, 2.physical, 3.chronic, 4.recommends, 5.preferably, 6.obtain', NULL, NULL),
  ('5e5faab2-a968-5fd1-8d8a-e17b0544a6e3'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'READ_ALOUD', E'READ_ALOUD', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '5e5faab2-a968-5fd1-8d8a-e17b0544a6e3'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Calendars', E'Archaeologists have reconstructed methods of timekeeping that go back to prehistoric times at least as old as the Neolithic. The natural units for timekeeping used by most historical societies are the day and the solar year. Calendars are explicit schemes used for timekeeping. The first historically attested calendars date to the Bronze Age, dependent on the development of writing in the ancient Near East.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
