  ('71fc5cce-282b-5c17-87af-4cda457ac21c'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '71fc5cce-282b-5c17-87af-4cda457ac21c'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Social Exchange Theory', E'Social exchange theory suggests that people try to {{1}} rewards and minimize costs in social relationships. Each person entering the marriage market comes {{2}} with assets and liabilities or a certain amount of social currency with which to attract a prospective mate. For some people, assets might include earning {{3}} and status, while for others, assets might include physical attractiveness and youth.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.maximize, 2.equipped, 3.potential', NULL, NULL),
  ('71fc6cb7-b84a-50ac-a4ea-891728541a90'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '71fc6cb7-b84a-50ac-a4ea-891728541a90'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Environmental Policy', E'Thus the environmental policy does not contribute to the profitability in any real sense at all. In practice it is companies that are well organized and {{1}} , or that are already comfortably profitable, that have time to {{2}} and police environmental policies. However, if someone says that profitable companies are the ones most likely to consider environment, this is confusing cause with effect. It is not that environmental best {{3}} causes profitability, but that being profitable allows for {{4}} for the environment.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.efficient, 2.establish, 3.practice, 4.concern', NULL, NULL),
  ('71fe045e-720a-5e12-b47f-889bb8aef3b0'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '71fe045e-720a-5e12-b47f-889bb8aef3b0'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'WFD', NULL, 'e85d8ee9-c472-503b-96af-64a5669efd22'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'The student advisor was aware that today''s lecture was not happening.', NULL, NULL),
  ('7200dadf-7d24-5e9b-b667-ca8cf1335b5b'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '7200dadf-7d24-5e9b-b667-ca8cf1335b5b'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Satellite', E'Satellite data shows that the Earth has increased its green leaf area by 5 percent in the last two decades, and the rate of greening is accelerating across more than half of its land. This phenomenon, known as “global greening”, might seem like good news for the environment, but it is not as simple as it {{1}} . One of the main drivers of global greening is the rise of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere, which {{2}} as a fertilizer for plants. However, this also means that the planet is warming up due to the greenhouse effect, which has many negative consequences for ecosystems and human well-being. Another driver is the expansion of agriculture and tree planting, especially in China and India, which {{3}} for one-third of all greening. While these activities can increase food production and forest cover, they also often replace native habitats, reduce biodiversity, and consume water and chemicals. {{4}} , global greening is not a sign that nature is healing, but rather that humans are altering the environment on a planetary {{5}} . The changing color of the Earth reflects the complex and sometimes contradictory impacts of human activities on the biosphere.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.sounds, 2.acts, 3.account, 4.Therefore, 5.scale', NULL, NULL),
  ('72013ee9-b480-531a-861a-1e4f3d4fe4e9'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'MC_READING_MULTIPLE', E'MC_READING_MULTIPLE', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '72013ee9-b480-531a-861a-1e4f3d4fe4e9'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Neue National Gallery', E'The Neue National Gallery in Berlin was designed by Mies van der Rohe. Built in 1968, it is a departure from the traditional museum concept of a closed building with exhibition rooms. Instead it is an open-plan, flexible space.\nWith only two steel columns on each side, the corners are ''free'', giving the building a lightweight look.\nThe gallery was the first building completed as part of a duster of buildings dedicated to culture and the fine arts. It is often said that the building is a work of art in itself. The unusual natural illumination in the building, coming from around and below the viewer rather than above, has the effect of shocking the viewer out of their usual way of seeing and encouraging visitors to bring a fresh eye to the art.\nThe upper level is mainly used for special exhibits, for example, large-scale sculptures or paintings. The vast lower level has space for themed shows, and contains shops, a cafe, and the museum''s permanent collection, which ranges from early modern art to art of the 1960s. The podium roof plaza is an open air gallery for public sculpture.\nOn request, guests may enter the garden to see figurative and abstract sculptures on display there. Special exhibits are attended by specially trained, very personable staff, who field questions, explain the exhibits, and enthuse about their favourite works.\n\nWhat does the passage say can be found at the Neue National Gallery?', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'artwork on top of the building\nhelpful guides to give information about the art', NULL, NULL),
  ('720869e8-54fc-5fbd-9547-0343652c4222'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '720869e8-54fc-5fbd-9547-0343652c4222'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'RS', NULL, '1a064a6e-0be4-594c-aa90-cf3073154fea'::uuid, NULL, E'History gives us the tools to analyze and explain problems in the past.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
