  ('772a83f5-3b8b-5fdb-85ab-d3af60745470'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '772a83f5-3b8b-5fdb-85ab-d3af60745470'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'ASQ', E'What do we call a festival which is held every four years gathering people together as a sporting event?', '882f070a-38d8-5b97-9476-2a9a52bfe452'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'The Olympic Games / Olympics', NULL, NULL),
  ('772c3791-b4ba-5a6d-b71c-f2c339128775'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '772c3791-b4ba-5a6d-b71c-f2c339128775'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Musical Education', E'Since the dawn of civilization, music has always been an indispensable part of children''s education. Today, in most countries around the world, music courses are still {{1}} in schools as part of the basic curriculum. The ancient Greeks believed that music should be {{2}} alongside mathematics and physics, as these subjects complement and enhance one another.\nResearchers at The Ohio State University conducted a retrospective study on the behavior of children (mainly high school students) who had been {{3}} to music courses in their early years or throughout their school life. The presence of music in adolescent education has a significant impact on their ability to {{4}} complex concepts such as mathematics or physics.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.offered, 2.performed, 3.exposed, 4.understand', NULL, NULL),
  ('772f419c-1bbf-5f85-9244-46c3ba5047ce'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '772f419c-1bbf-5f85-9244-46c3ba5047ce'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'ASQ', E'What is the food that is used in a recipe?', '390a8c95-9aa3-507d-99ee-dde0ee55a3ac'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Ingredient', NULL, NULL),
  ('77383f35-436a-53ca-9970-c051e5b80e2f'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '77383f35-436a-53ca-9970-c051e5b80e2f'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Hesitation Phenomenon', NULL, '4fac0251-8573-56c1-8623-df2f53980153'::uuid, NULL, E'While a written word might have multiple definitions, we can usually determine its intended meaning through context. In speech however, a word can take on additional layers of meaning. Tone of voice, the relationship between speakers, and expectations of where a conversation will go can imbue even words that seem like filler with vital information. This is where “um” and “uh” come in. Or “eh” and “ehm,” “tutoa” and “öö,” “eto” and “ano.” Linguists call these filled pauses, which are a kind of hesitation phenomenon. And these seemingly insignificant interruptions are actually quite meaningful in spoken communication. For example, while a silent pause might be interpreted as a sign for others to start speaking, a filled pause can signal that you’re not finished yet. Hesitation phenomena can buy time for your speech to catch up with your thoughts, or to fish out the right word for a situation. And they don’t just benefit the speaker — a filled pause lets your listeners know an important word is on the way. Linguists have even found that people are more likely to remember a word if it comes after a hesitation. Hesitation phenomena aren’t the only parts of speech that take on new meaning during dialogue. Words and phrases such as “like,” “well” or “you know” function as discourse markers, ignoring their literal meaning to convey something about the sentence in which they appear.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('773be7dc-e620-525d-91ce-121bbf7ed577'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '773be7dc-e620-525d-91ce-121bbf7ed577'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'ASQ', E'Whose job is making and repairing wooden objects and structures?', '1e48fd1c-7a74-5c24-8853-4772e8e3fefa'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Carpenter', NULL, NULL),
  ('773f9d8c-3c4e-5001-91f6-13261ee49de4'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '773f9d8c-3c4e-5001-91f6-13261ee49de4'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'WFD', NULL, '32d4b820-dc31-57bd-8d61-606a38ab777e'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'The city''s founders created a set of rules that became laws.', NULL, NULL),
  ('7740119e-8ccd-51d0-a464-79d9b1c10b86'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '7740119e-8ccd-51d0-a464-79d9b1c10b86'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Solar Cells', E'Recent breakthroughs in materials science have significantly enhanced the stability and efficiency of perovskite solar cells. Researchers have made strides in {{1}} the challenges traditionally associated with these materials, such as their sensitivity to environmental conditions, which previously {{2}} their practical application. Traditionally plagued by issues of stability and environmental sensitivity, perovskite materials are now at the {{3}} of solar energy research due to significant improvements in their design and composition. A team of researchers has recently achieved remarkable progress in enhancing both the efficiency and stability of these cells, addressing long-standing hurdles that have {{4}} their practical application. This leap forward not only promises to elevate the viability of perovskite solar cells in the competitive landscape of renewable energy technologies but also paves the way for more sustainable, cost-effective solar solutions.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.overcoming, 2.limited, 3.forefront, 4.impeded', NULL, NULL),
  ('774372bf-3c9b-5512-b3e4-7b1d85d1fcd1'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '774372bf-3c9b-5512-b3e4-7b1d85d1fcd1'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'RS', NULL, '239ba144-af66-574a-9aee-0f94a614e4c9'::uuid, NULL, E'Charles used to ride the bus to town, but now his roommate drives him in.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('7743b8b2-53e4-50a8-8435-2d5c7240b99a'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '7743b8b2-53e4-50a8-8435-2d5c7240b99a'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Holy Grail', E'It''s interesting that in our minds, we keep thinking of the {{1}} discovery like it''s the Holy Grail. But there are a couple of shortcuts here that I''d like to unpack. I''m not a doctor, I''m just a {{2}} . My clients focus on health care — biopharma companies, providers, global health {{3}} — and they''ve educated me. We need to find the tools to fight COVID, and we need to make them {{4}} to all.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.vaccine, 2.consultant, 3.institutions, 4.accessible', NULL, NULL),
  ('7743e626-a4e8-59a2-a85e-6f690e354b9d'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '7743e626-a4e8-59a2-a85e-6f690e354b9d'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'WFD', NULL, 'be4f05a9-0150-5cf9-b0d1-0465dab9fa83'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Assignments should be submitted before the specified deadline to avoid penalties.', NULL, NULL),
  ('7743f610-0773-5b5a-ae09-c7c25b3acaf0'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '7743f610-0773-5b5a-ae09-c7c25b3acaf0'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'ASQ', E'What is the term for a large mass of ice that moves slowly over land, typically in polar regions?', 'ee9d8dca-9b1a-55d9-8622-00089b55a924'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Glacier', NULL, NULL),
  ('77451edd-2a66-5aeb-8ef3-38b85c05aea6'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'SUMMARIZE_WRITTEN_TEXT', E'SUMMARIZE_WRITTEN_TEXT', E'WRITING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '77451edd-2a66-5aeb-8ef3-38b85c05aea6'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'House Mice', E'According to new research, house mice (Mus musculus) are ideal biomarkers of human settlement as they tend to stow away in crates or on ships that end up going where people go. Where people go, so do mice, often stowing away in carts of hay or on ships. Despite a natural range of just 100 meters (109 yards) and an evolutionary base near Pakistan, the house mouse has managed to colonize every continent, which makes it a useful tool for researchers like Searle.\nUsing mice as a proxy for human movement can add to what is already known through archaeological data and answer important questions in areas where there is a lack of artifacts, Searle said.\nPrevious research conducted by Searle at the University of York supported the theory that Australian mice originated in the British Isles and probably came over with convicts shipped there to colonize the continent in the late 18th and 19th centuries. He came to the conclusion by using evolutionary techniques to analyze mitochondrial DNA, comparing modern-day mouse populations from Australia with those from their likely regional source in Western Europe. In the Viking study, he and his fellow researchers in Iceland, Denmark, and Sweden took it a step further, using ancient mouse DNA collected from archaeological sites dating from the 10th to 12th centuries, as well as modern mice. He is hoping to do just that in his next project, which involves tracking the migration of mice and other species, including plants, across the Indian Ocean, from South Asia to East Africa.', NULL, NULL, E'House mice are ideal biomarkers of human settlement, as using mice as a proxy for human movement can add to what is already known, and the house mouse has managed to colonize every continent, when Australian mice originated in the British Isles and probably came over with convicts, so Searle and his fellow researchers took it a step further, using ancient mouse DNA collected from archaeological sites.', NULL, 5, 75),
  ('77505927-ac4b-55db-af9a-23523c6c0d11'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'READ_ALOUD', E'READ_ALOUD', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '77505927-ac4b-55db-af9a-23523c6c0d11'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Coastal Wetlands', E'The coastal wetlands have environmental and economic importance. Wetlands provide natural wealth. They have important filtering capabilities. As the runoff water passes, they retain excess nutrients and some pollutants. They maintain water flow during dry periods. Thousands of people depend on groundwater for drinking. They act as natural sponges of flood waters and contain soil erosion. They control floods and save the buildings from collapsing during heavy rains.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('77586f00-5790-59ea-bd6b-923f7d5ce972'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '77586f00-5790-59ea-bd6b-923f7d5ce972'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Green Turn', NULL, 'd70b654e-89c5-5e1b-a945-302ff3872c4c'::uuid, NULL, E'I kept thinking: Why does the item have to be returned to the retailer in the first place? What if there was another way, a win-win for everyone? What if when a person is trying to return something, it could go to the next shopper who wants it, and not the retailer? What if, instead of a return, they could do what I call a “green turn”? Consumers could use an app to take pictures of the item and verify the condition while returning it. Artificial intelligence systems could then sort these clothes by condition — mint condition or slightly used — and direct it to the next appropriate person. Mint-condition clothes could automatically go to the next buyer, while slightly used clothes could be marked down and offered online again. The retailer can decide the business rules on the number of times a particular item can be resold. All that the consumer would need to do is obtain a mobile code, take it to the nearest shipping place to be packed and shipped, and off it goes from one buyer to the next, not the landfill. Now you will ask, “Would people really go through all this trouble?” I think they would if they had incentives, like loyalty points or cash back. Let’s call it “green cash.” There would be a whole new opportunity to make money from this new customer base looking to buy these returns. This system would make a fun thing like shopping a spiritual experience that helps save our planet.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('77630bf3-16d4-5b97-bd9b-85ad0f39fb43'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '77630bf3-16d4-5b97-bd9b-85ad0f39fb43'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'WFD', NULL, 'e678970c-45a2-5ccd-aa9b-06ad1947f990'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Measures must be taken to prevent unemployment rate from increasing.', NULL, NULL),
  ('776a4a06-0a2d-575b-84ef-0160f8b46f1c'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '776a4a06-0a2d-575b-84ef-0160f8b46f1c'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'WFD', NULL, '9c563a30-f8ea-5bec-8c68-dfda0de57fb9'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'You should submit your term papers to the general office.', NULL, NULL),
  ('776af6ba-4b99-50a9-a914-553fa550644a'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '776af6ba-4b99-50a9-a914-553fa550644a'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Sign Languages', E'Evidence suggests ancient Egyptians and Greeks used hand gestures to supplement communication. Yet, it wasn''t {{1}} the 17th century in Europe that formal sign languages began to flourish. Institutions for the deaf {{2}} , and with them, educators like Charles-Michel de l''Épée in France, who developed systems using manual alphabets and gestures. Like languages spoken, sign languages diversified. American Sign Language (ASL), for instance, branched out from French Sign Language, carrying echoes of its European cousin but forging its own {{3}} identity. This evolution wasn''t confined to geography. Within communities, nuances emerged, reflecting regional dialects and cultural experiences. Deaf communities thrived, stories weaving through generations, jokes punctuated by laughter translated into expressive movements. However, the {{4}} wasn''t paved with only sunshine. The 19th century saw the rise of oralism, a push to prioritize spoken language for the deaf. Sign languages were deemed inferior, relegating them to the shadows. Thankfully, the pendulum swung back. The late 20th century {{5}} a resurgence of sign languages, recognized as complete and complex systems with rich grammar and expressive power.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.until, 2.emerged, 3.unique, 4.path, 5.witnessed', NULL, NULL),
  ('7770e351-326d-5cf4-9479-b60aee1ef9cc'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'SUMMARIZE_WRITTEN_TEXT', E'SUMMARIZE_WRITTEN_TEXT', E'WRITING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '7770e351-326d-5cf4-9479-b60aee1ef9cc'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Public Figures'' Privacy', E'Public figures include politicians and other public officials such as judges and civil servants as well as celebrities such as film stars, musicians and sports stars. The very nature of these roles opens these people to scrutiny by the press. The extent to which the media are legally free to investigate and publish details of public figures'' private lives varies from country to country. Countries like France are much stricter on protection personal privacy than, for example, Britain.\nPeople have a right to know about those in power. Whether through taxes, in the case of politicians and civil servants, or by revenue generated by films, TV, sports appearances or concerts in the case of celebrities – these people''s income is dependent on the general public.\nPeople have the right to make informed judgements about the kind of leaders they have. Attempts to restrict what may be reported about public figures in the press could easily become a conspiracy to keep voters in the dark and to manipulate them. All elections are to a greater or lesser extent about the character of the leading politicians involved. Unless the voters are allowed insights into their private lives they will lack the information needed to make a fair decision at the polling booth. For example, some people believe that a politician who betrayed his wife in an affair was equally capable of breaking his promises and lying to his country.\nExposing corruption and dishonesty on the part of public officials and businesses is a critical part of the function of a free press, and it is essential to the functioning of a free-market economy. If investigative journalists are prevented from scrutinising the private lives of public figures, then corruption and crime will be much easier to hide.\nPublic figures know that with fame comes a price and that price is constant scrutiny. In fact, many celebrities actively seek media exposure in order to advance their careers, revealing to the median many aspects of their personal lives. Once success has been bought in such a fashion it is then somewhat hypocritical to complain of "press intrusion" into those few aspects the star would prefer to remain hidden.', NULL, NULL, E'Public figures'' very nature opens them to scrutiny by the press, with the extent to which the media are legally free to investigate and publish details of public figures’ private lives varying from country to country, and people have a right to know about them to make informed judgements about the kind of leaders they have, because exposing corruption and dishonesty of public figures is a critical part of the function of a press.', NULL, 5, 75),
  ('77737d1e-aab7-5e73-8ea4-5a92f81cef30'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '77737d1e-aab7-5e73-8ea4-5a92f81cef30'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'ASQ', E'Which source is more reliable, magazine or journal?', '1a935a98-d52e-5401-8952-b6c518d1deb4'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Journal', NULL, NULL),
  ('7778f640-7488-5c8b-ac2d-dab014108e7d'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '7778f640-7488-5c8b-ac2d-dab014108e7d'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'WFD', NULL, '1821f9e0-a5e9-500f-aab2-50ee53f533de'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Education and training provide important skills for the labor force.', NULL, NULL),
  ('777aff29-d70c-5a11-917d-eb61b4432db4'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'READ_ALOUD', E'READ_ALOUD', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '777aff29-d70c-5a11-917d-eb61b4432db4'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Goods and Services', E'Quite obviously, a significant rise in the number of people in a given area or country will affect the demand for a whole host of goods and services. Note that a change in the structure of the population will increase the demand for some goods but reduce the demand for others.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('777c1588-4edc-5875-9ae6-6f155a895e80'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'SUMMARIZE_WRITTEN_TEXT', E'SUMMARIZE_WRITTEN_TEXT', E'WRITING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '777c1588-4edc-5875-9ae6-6f155a895e80'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Sea Creatures', E'Most sea creatures, from whales and dolphins to fish, sharks, shrimps and possibly even anemones respond to sound, and many can produce it. They use it to hunt and to hide, find mates and food, form and guide shoals, navigate ''blind'', send messages and transmit warnings, establish territories, warn off competitors, stun prey, deceive predators, and sense changes in water and conditions.\nMarine animals click bones and grind teeth; use drum-tight bladders and special sonic organs to chirp, grunt, and boom: belch gases; and vibrate special organs. Far from the ''silent deep'', the oceans are a raucous babel.\nInto this age-long tumult, in the blink of an evolutionary eye, has entered a new thunder: the throb of mighty engines as 46,220 large vessels plough the world''s shipping lanes. Scientists say that background noise in the ocean has increased roughly by 15 decibels in the past 50 years. It may not sound like much in overall terms; but it is enough, according to many marine biologists, to mask the normal sounds of ocean life going about its business. At its most intense, some even say noise causes whales to become disoriented, dolphins to develop ''the bends'', fish to go deaf, flee their breeding grounds or fail to form shoals - enough to disrupt the basic biology of two thirds of the planet.\n''Undersea noise pollution is like the death of a thousand cuts'', says Sylvia Earle, chief scientist of the US National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration. ''Each sound in itself may not be a matter of critical concern, but taken all together,the noise from shipping,seismic surveys, and military activity is creating a totally different environment than existed even 50 years ago. That high level of noise is bound to have a hard, sweeping impact on life in the sea.', NULL, NULL, E'Many ocean creatures are dependent on underwater sound to live and breed, but in the last 50 years noises made by humans from ship engines, seismic research, and military activity have increased background ocean noise levels by about 15 decibels, which may have a very negative and disruptive effect on sea life when its impact is considered altogether.', NULL, 5, 75),
  ('777e7c10-ed72-583d-b388-86a9edc53fd6'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '777e7c10-ed72-583d-b388-86a9edc53fd6'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'WFD', NULL, 'c8711c1b-55d1-55e3-8117-b439e3781449'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'The organization has been staffed by unpaid volunteers.', NULL, NULL),
