  ('7b3efed3-4a89-56d6-8016-1c0514cbfb99'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '7b3efed3-4a89-56d6-8016-1c0514cbfb99'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'WFD', NULL, 'a26f1ed9-ae80-5245-aea5-4064f5cd61f8'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Discover how to access your resources before beginning your research.', NULL, NULL),
  ('7b407c4e-c064-5024-97dd-0a2518ffbec7'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '7b407c4e-c064-5024-97dd-0a2518ffbec7'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'RS', NULL, 'a6371bd3-f62f-5b7c-8bed-cac7d90677d1'::uuid, NULL, E'Please use the button below if you want to reserve a computer.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('7b43a7dc-0664-59ec-8984-34548a755c06'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'MC_LISTENING_MULTIPLE', E'MC_LISTENING_MULTIPLE', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '7b43a7dc-0664-59ec-8984-34548a755c06'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Salmon', E'Listen to a discussion in an ecology class. The class is talking about the salmons run various species of pacific salmon make a round trip from the small streams where they are born to the sea and then back to the stream of their origin, where they spawn and die. This round trip is known as the salmons run. The end of the salmons run is the beginning of the next generation, pacific salmon hatch on the head waters of a stream as fry. The fish then migrate down stream via rivers and eventually to the ocean, where they require several years to mature while in the sea, salmon from many river systems school and feed together. When mature the salmon form into groups of common geographic origin and migrate back toward the river. They emerge from this juveniles. Is it true that they find their way home by their sense of smell? During the first stage of their return? They navigate by the position of the sun. But later when they reach the river leading to their home stream, their keen sense of smell takes over. Just what is it? They can smell the other fish. The water flowing from each stream carries a unique scent. This scent comes from the types of plants, soil, and other components of that stream. This centers apparently imprinted in the memory of a salmon fry before it migrate to the c I remember having a real shock when I was hiking. Once I was looking at a waterfall and I saw a salmon jump up about ten feet. At first, I couldn''t believe my eyes, but then I saw another one do it, and then several more. It was an awesome sight. They must have an incredibly powerful instinct. The survival of their species depends on their ability to get home and reproduce. And of course, other species depend on the survival of the salmon. Salmon provide an important link in the food chain. They spend ninety percent of their lives in the ocean, where they feed on plankton, shrimp and small fish. When they make their return journey, they carry nutrients from the ocean back to the rivers and streams. Up north, where I used to live in the river valley, the eagles would gather for the salmon run. Every year. They gorge themselves. And all the salmon that had just spawned nothing is wasted in nature. After the salmon spawn their caucuses feed birds, mammals and vegetation, and even their own newly hatched offspring.\n\nHow do salmon find their way to their home stream?', 'c3bdf95c-7497-536a-8ef2-446804bc6769'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'By seeing the sun''s position\nBy smelling the water', NULL, NULL),
  ('7b471171-a356-5aa0-a710-22baac3c5264'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '7b471171-a356-5aa0-a710-22baac3c5264'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Paris', E'For centuries Paris has been one of the world’s most important and attractive cities. It is {{1}} for the opportunities it offers for business and commerce, for study, for culture, and for entertainment; its gastronomy, painting, literature, and intellectual community especially enjoy an {{2}} reputation. Its sobriquet ‘the City of Light’, earned during the Enlightenment, remains {{3}} , for Paris has {{4}} its importance as a center for education and intellectual pursuits.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.appreciated, 2.enviable, 3.appropriate, 4.retained', NULL, NULL),
  ('7b505db3-dcda-5aa7-8e2c-0d6de4505c20'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '7b505db3-dcda-5aa7-8e2c-0d6de4505c20'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'WFD', NULL, 'a3a57b44-2978-51fe-9d1b-8c3508d3f42c'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'The most popular courses still have a few places left.', NULL, NULL),
  ('7b52a150-603a-59a5-a2f7-8ae140a785d6'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'SUMMARIZE_WRITTEN_TEXT', E'SUMMARIZE_WRITTEN_TEXT', E'WRITING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '7b52a150-603a-59a5-a2f7-8ae140a785d6'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Coordination Changes', E'Changes in coordination are less related to muscles and more related to the brain and nervous system. Multiple brain centers need to be well coordinated to allow you to do everything from hitting a golf ball to keeping a coffee cup steady as you walk across a room. This means that the wiring of the brain, the so-called white matter that connects the different brain regions, is crucial.\nUnfortunately, most people in our society over age 60 who eat a western diet and don’t get enough exercise have some tiny "ministrokes" (also called microvascular or small vessel disease) in their white matter. Although the strokes are so small that they are not noticeable when they occur, they can disrupt the connections between important brain coordination centers such as the frontal lobe (which directs movements) and the cerebellum (which provides on-the-fly corrections to those movements as needed).\nIn addition, losing dopamine-producing cells is common as you get older, which can slow down your movements and reduce your coordination, so even if you don’t develop Parkinson’s disease, many people develop some of the abnormalities in movement seen in Parkinson''s.\nLastly, changes in vision — the "eye" side of hand-eye coordination — are also important. Eye diseases are much more common in older adults, including cataracts, glaucoma, and macular degeneration. In addition, mild difficulty seeing can be the first sign of cognitive disorders of aging, including Lewy body disease and Alzheimer’s.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 5, 75),
  ('7b55a936-a0ed-5d8c-9c9c-2f665b16c66d'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'READ_ALOUD', E'READ_ALOUD', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '7b55a936-a0ed-5d8c-9c9c-2f665b16c66d'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'The Astrophysics Division', E'In the Science Mission Directorate, the Astrophysics Division studies the universe. The science goals of this division are breathtaking: we seek to understand the universe and our place in it. We are starting to investigate the very moment of creation of the universe and are close to learning the full history of stars and galaxies. We are discovering how planetary systems form and how environments develop.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('7b59ecea-9e11-56e2-80d6-6e25f7a60fc3'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'MC_LISTENING_SINGLE', E'MC_LISTENING_SINGLE', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '7b59ecea-9e11-56e2-80d6-6e25f7a60fc3'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Composition', E'To listen to an art instructor talk about composition. Composition is the organization of shapes and forms into a hole and expressive hole. The elements of composition, line shape, tone and color need to be well arranged, need to be ordered, they need to be coherent, just like the words and phrases and sentences in a piece of writing. All paintings have a compositional element. Successful paintings sort of suggest the third dimension, the sense that the design goes beyond the picture frame a pictures. Unity, which includes the shapes, tones, and colors, is linked to what the artist has to say. The artists message is strongest when it''s clear, a composition is better. If it says one thing strongly, then if it tries to say too many things, a crowded composition is sort of fussy and splintered, and lacks unity. Even a painting of a single object needs thoughtful composition. So the character of the object is present in every shape.\n\nWhat does the instructor imply about composition?', '87da7dca-f9e7-5f98-93b1-35bd85f1c280'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Composition in painting is similar to composition in writing.', NULL, NULL),
  ('7b5e66a6-5538-5c14-8d7a-5b8cc693af90'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '7b5e66a6-5538-5c14-8d7a-5b8cc693af90'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'WFD', NULL, 'a97448a0-0790-5210-878a-531a4eaefc70'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'The same approach reached the same explanation of the problem.', NULL, NULL),
  ('7b682298-c352-5b00-af2c-2b161a1586a1'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '7b682298-c352-5b00-af2c-2b161a1586a1'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Lens', E'The lens in the human eye works much like the lens in a camera. Just as a camera {{1}} its focus, the lens changes its shape to bend and focus the light a second time, ensuring that you have a clear and {{2}} image of whatever you are looking at. Whether the object is close or {{3}} away, the lens adapts accordingly. Structurally, the lens is made up of water and protein, which together allow it to remain transparent and flexible throughout much of life.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.adjusts, 2.precise, 3.far', NULL, NULL),
  ('7b68e2cd-e1d0-51cb-bd75-42496707889e'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '7b68e2cd-e1d0-51cb-bd75-42496707889e'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'ASQ', E'What do things become when they come to a halt?', '17652733-f129-5160-b562-57fcfc9db44d'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Stationary / static / pause / quiescence', NULL, NULL),
  ('7b6d4abb-8198-5fac-a67b-9d97f66c309f'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '7b6d4abb-8198-5fac-a67b-9d97f66c309f'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'ASQ', E'How many sides is a peninsula surrounded by water from?', '1cb4e62b-3245-5943-b81a-c545f0a04fbb'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Three', NULL, NULL),
  ('7b6dd92e-bcfc-5496-89b9-8f273af45865'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '7b6dd92e-bcfc-5496-89b9-8f273af45865'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Zebras', E'Zebras in the open African savanna had many more predators to worry about, so natural selection has {{1}} them into very reactive animals that are ready to {{2}} at the slightest sign of danger. Also zebras have no family structure and no hierarchy, so despite their horse-like appearance, zebras won’t {{3}} easily to humans.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.forged, 2.leap, 3.submit', NULL, NULL),
  ('7b70a1da-e267-528a-8bdf-303c27f88626'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'MC_LISTENING_SINGLE', E'MC_LISTENING_SINGLE', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '7b70a1da-e267-528a-8bdf-303c27f88626'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Industry of Cell', E'Through to listen to part of a talk in a science class. As you recall from our previous discussion, the chemistry of life is organized into metabolic pathways. Next year, in your organic chemistry lab, you''ll go into this into metabolism in more depth. Since this is an intro course, you need only a general understanding of the process. For now, there''s a wonderful video tape i''d like you to know about that will help you review for the test next week. It''s part of the transformation series that was on television about a year ago. The episode you should watch is called the industry of a cell. I strongly urge you to see it. I believe our library has more than one copy. It shows lots of examples. The many ways that cells use energy for metabolism. For example, it shows how bacteria in the headlight of a certain fish, how these bacteria take the energy stored in food and converted into light in a process called bioluminescence. You should all try to see this program before next week. I highly recommend it. In fact, you can expect to see examples from it on the test.\n\nWhat does the professor imply about the videotape?', 'fe7e6200-8dcb-5843-8cae-e6a61aa3f50b'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'It covers material that will be on the next test.', NULL, NULL),
  ('7b79462f-8908-5d44-87a1-916e888b728c'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'READ_ALOUD', E'READ_ALOUD', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '7b79462f-8908-5d44-87a1-916e888b728c'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Cow Feed', E'More than half of cow feed is actually grass, and farmers call it hay and silage. While people often think dairy cows are fed a high-grain diet, in reality they eat the leaves and stems from corn, wheat and oats far more often than they are eating grain, like corn kernels.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('7b794a7a-57be-59f5-9d75-d4cc6f093711'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '7b794a7a-57be-59f5-9d75-d4cc6f093711'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'WFD', NULL, '7c4491c2-3e1a-510f-873a-2381f5d3ca36'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'The library will stay open until midnight this week.', NULL, NULL),
  ('7b7c9ec5-c1e3-53e5-a577-76a73fa921db'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '7b7c9ec5-c1e3-53e5-a577-76a73fa921db'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'ASQ', E'What do we call the citrus fruit that''s a hybrid between a pomelo and an orange?', '84a1f26e-fd36-5b0a-bb10-4525e4dfbf6f'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Grapefruit', NULL, NULL),
  ('7b7e5b8a-fab6-5553-b288-aa7b45644513'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '7b7e5b8a-fab6-5553-b288-aa7b45644513'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'RS', NULL, '13e249f9-a451-53c3-873c-b8cf9a424292'::uuid, NULL, E'The people of the remote village showed me great hospitality.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('7b8a12cc-64d1-5836-8634-96879506613c'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '7b8a12cc-64d1-5836-8634-96879506613c'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Protected Areas', E'"Protected areas are hugely important for {{1}} . Over the last 20 years there''s been a massive growth in the number of protected areas, both in the marine realm and the terrestrial realm."\nJames Watson with the Wildlife Conservation Society''s Global Conservation Program and the University of Queensland.\n"But what we''re noticing is that these protected areas aren''t well placed. They don''t really stop {{2}} loss in many places, because they''re not where important areas of biodiversity is. We also notice that protected areas aren''t well managed and therefore often protected areas don''t {{3}} lead to any significant conservation outcome."\nTo address these issues Watson and colleagues published a blueprint describing how "the scientific community can actually really help national governments and protected area {{4}} really place protected areas that really {{5}} important biodiversity areas and achieve ecological representation."\nTheir report is in the journal Conservation Biology.\n"Protected areas aren''t the only tool in the toolbox and we''ve got to make sure that other efforts actually work together holistically with protected areas. So scientists can help {{6}} metrics that help allow us to assess this. In these three ways, scientists can really help the conservation {{7}} ensure that the protected areas are in the right place and managed effectively and can achieve conservation outcomes in the long-term."', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.conservation, 2.biodiversity, 3.actually, 4.managers, 5.prioritize, 6.identify, 7.movement', NULL, NULL),
  ('7b959546-9e6f-5d12-b565-23af282132a0'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'READ_ALOUD', E'READ_ALOUD', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '7b959546-9e6f-5d12-b565-23af282132a0'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Light Speed', E'As far as we know, nothing can move faster than light. According to Einstein''s theories on relativity, it takes an infinite amount of energy to accelerate an object with mass up to the speed of light. As a result, it would appear that having a spacecraft traveling at or exceeding the speed of light is simply impossible.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('7b99f63a-a647-5cb0-9b53-e5702b6d9cc4'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '7b99f63a-a647-5cb0-9b53-e5702b6d9cc4'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'WFD', NULL, 'ab53315c-39eb-5054-a57f-814f367b84b4'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Theatre courses really encourage students to be more creative.', NULL, NULL),
  ('7b9a404b-05a8-5a8a-b2e4-5c2fd44589e8'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '7b9a404b-05a8-5a8a-b2e4-5c2fd44589e8'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Intuition', NULL, 'a8ca603f-db1a-5407-8006-dc3670e63d60'::uuid, NULL, E'English philosopher John Locke gives us a pretty standard way to map out boundaries of intuition. So that’s where we’ll start. Locke contrasts intuition to sensory perception on one side and to demonstration on the other. Sensory perception, he notices, is always about particular things. You see this pizza in front of you right now. Maybe you see that this pizza is round. But we aren’t restricted to making judgements about particular things. When we judge that “No round things are square”, we aren’t just thinking about that particular pizza, but about a more general and abstract truth. Judging that circles are different from squares, according to Locke, is intuitive. And at least in this kind of case where Locke thinks we’re recognizing features of our ideas, intuition is a perfectly good source of knowledge. We know that no round things are square through intuition. Locke also draws a contrast between intuition and demonstration. Intuition can tell us directly that a circle is not a triangle. But when we get to more complex questions, we need to use demonstration or explicit reasoning. So for example, we can figure out that the interior angles of a triangle add up to two right angles, but we have to go through a series of steps to gain this knowledge. And that’s demonstration. Demonstration requires conscious stages. Intuition is immediate. Locke notices that intuition and demonstration are connected, however. Each individual step in a chain of demonstrative reasoning is, or at least should be, intuitive. Contemporary thinkers still draw a similar distinction using a variety of labels for it.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('7bab594e-06b3-592a-ac32-9dc3745455d2'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '7bab594e-06b3-592a-ac32-9dc3745455d2'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'ASQ', E'Who is in charge of a ship?', 'f044a15c-56ee-5796-ae41-6e4ab8900d58'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Captain', NULL, NULL),
  ('7baf9a00-5c80-5ddd-83cb-0874c391bb52'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '7baf9a00-5c80-5ddd-83cb-0874c391bb52'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'WFD', NULL, '2841e8f8-9005-566d-b539-1e973cd50ead'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'We must act to prevent an increase in the unemployment rate.', NULL, NULL),
  ('7bb11149-a851-55e1-be4c-14d8d49f46ad'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '7bb11149-a851-55e1-be4c-14d8d49f46ad'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'WFD', NULL, 'af1783a5-eaa3-5bbb-bc1b-ff4d4a751f26'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Learning a foreign language takes a lot of effort.', NULL, NULL),
  ('7bb2588b-a4c2-5926-a96a-8d49804d0865'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '7bb2588b-a4c2-5926-a96a-8d49804d0865'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'WFD', NULL, '9936ac88-ed04-59fb-aa43-02f1aec87294'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Years of training are required to become a medical specialist.', NULL, NULL),
  ('7bb46ef6-708a-5d09-8e9f-cc4df1e9a0c3'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'READ_ALOUD', E'READ_ALOUD', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '7bb46ef6-708a-5d09-8e9f-cc4df1e9a0c3'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Long-distance Fliers', E'The researchers think that long-distance fliers such as the American golden-plover and the white-rumped sandpiper picked up the spores while lining their nests. Then when the birds arrive in new places they molt, leaving behind the feathers and their precious cargo-to start growing again at the other end of the world.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
