  ('7ef30597-dbaf-58bd-979e-738dbc5d2de8'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '7ef30597-dbaf-58bd-979e-738dbc5d2de8'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Mass Migration', E'Mass migration has produced a huge worldwide economy of its own which has {{1}} so fast during the past few years that the figures have {{2}} experts. Last year remittances sent home by migrants were expected to {{3}} $232 billion according to the World Bank which {{4}} these vital figures through the flow of remittances and is to alleviate the plight of migrants'' families. Those who study the {{5}} of remittances argue that money allows poor countries to put off basic decisions of economic management like reforming their tax collection systems and building schools.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.accelerated, 2.astonished, 3.exceed, 4.tracks, 5.impacts', NULL, NULL),
  ('7ef9a959-60e2-5e2c-a96a-23522fb1d7b6'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '7ef9a959-60e2-5e2c-a96a-23522fb1d7b6'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Panic-striken Climate', E'First, the scientific community that studies climate change is quietly panic-stricken, because things are moving {{1}} faster than they expected. Greenhouse gas emissions are going up faster than {{2}} both from industrializing countries in Asia and from melting permafrost in Siberia and Canada. The Arctic Sea ice is melting so fast that the whole ocean may be ice-free in late summer in five years'' time. Most climate scientists now see last year''s report of the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change, whose forecasts are used by most governments for planning purposes, as a {{3}} historical document. Second, the biggest early impact of global warming will be on the food supply, both locally and globally. When the global average temperature hits one and a half degrees hotter - and it will; the carbon dioxide already in the atmosphere {{4}} us to that much warming - some countries will no longer be able to feed their people. Others, further from the equator, will still have enough food for themselves, but none to {{5}} .', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.much, 2.predicted, 3.purely, 4.commits, 5.spare', NULL, NULL),
