  ('9675dbf4-4f33-5f91-bf7c-92a29437d477'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '9675dbf4-4f33-5f91-bf7c-92a29437d477'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Papal Reform', E'Since the last papal reform, several {{1}} have been {{2}} to make the Western calendar more useful or {{3}} . Very few reforms, such as the rather different decimal French Republican and Soviet calendars, had gained official {{4}} , but each was put out of use shortly after its introduction.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.proposals, 2.offered, 3.regular, 4.acceptance', NULL, NULL),
  ('9677f06c-6918-5860-a1fc-62c9819e6718'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '9677f06c-6918-5860-a1fc-62c9819e6718'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Speedy Animals', E'Fast-moving animals – especially small ones, creatures that fly and top ocean predators – {{1}} time more quickly than others. That is, they can process more frames per second than slow-moving animals lower in the food chain, such as starfish, according to a comparison of more than 100 species. “We already know that different animals perceive time differently from us,” says Kevin Healy at the University of Galway in Ireland, who {{2}} the results at a meeting of the British Ecological Society on 20 December. But he wanted to find out, “If you’re a predator, do you have faster eyes than if you’re an herbivore?” He and his colleagues began by reviewing previously published research on the flicker fusion test, a common measure of the rate at which animals perceive the {{3}} of time. During the test, researchers increase the frequency of a flashing light until an animal sees it as a continuous glow, indicated by the reaction of light receptors in the animal’s retina.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.perceive, 2.presented, 3.passage', NULL, NULL),
  ('96851ed9-ac2b-5536-ba70-04990f975e76'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', '96851ed9-ac2b-5536-ba70-04990f975e76'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Effective Leader', E'In search of lessons to {{1}} in our own careers, we often try to {{2}} what effective leaders do. Roger Martin says this focus is misplaced, because moves that work in one context may make little sense in another. A more productive, though more difficult, approach is to look at how such leaders {{3}} . After extensive interviews with more than 50 of them, the author discovered that most are {{4}} thinkers -that is, they can hold in their heads two opposing ideas at once and then come up with a new idea that contains elements of {{5}} but is superior to both.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.apply, 2.emulate, 3.think, 4.integrative, 5.each', NULL, NULL),
