  ('afdc689b-6897-5fa7-b7b0-8f17d97f9733'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'afdc689b-6897-5fa7-b7b0-8f17d97f9733'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'WFD', NULL, 'cd78f8d9-5785-5e5f-a2b0-64b525e123ba'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Weather forecasting is becoming more accurate than before.', NULL, NULL),
  ('afdd569e-bbe6-53e6-84aa-f1357ae3cd0f'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RE_ORDER_PARAGRAPHS', E'RE_ORDER_PARAGRAPHS', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'afdd569e-bbe6-53e6-84aa-f1357ae3cd0f'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Gender Stereotype', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, E'3, 1, 2, 4, 5', NULL, NULL),
  ('afdde079-d63d-5edc-ab94-991bf15d14bb'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'afdde079-d63d-5edc-ab94-991bf15d14bb'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'RS', NULL, 'fe366101-7029-561a-8503-4853c1fb19ec'::uuid, NULL, E'It was a groundbreaking discovery, but they had to pay a very high price for it.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('afdf1614-af4d-5ef7-ba68-5b8fd4e5a6c8'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'HIGHLIGHT_CORRECT_SUMMARY', E'HIGHLIGHT_CORRECT_SUMMARY', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'afdf1614-af4d-5ef7-ba68-5b8fd4e5a6c8'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'HCS', E'I want to take a quick look at how material culture, that is, the things or objects we value and use, tools, technology, etc., affects the non- material culture, our customs, behavior, beliefs, attitudes, and so on. Now, of course, it works both ways: if you were, say, eighteen years old when mobile phones became widespread or when almost every home had a computer, you must have noticed a fairly significant change in people''s attitudes and behavior, including your own. These two technological innovations were readily accepted and considered very much a positive contribution to culture.\nOn the other hand, there is the way in which our attitudes and beliefs have an effect on the material culture. Take, for example, genetic science. While many, if not most, people welcome the advances made in this field - from the discovery of DNA to the genome project and cracking the genetic code, with its promise of finding cures for diseases that have plagued mankind throughout history - many, too, object to the idea of cloning. This is not just because they fear, without reason, that it will mean creating a race of identical people if it gets into the wrong hands, but simply because it is, in their opinion, unnatural. It goes against everything they believe in. Similarly, there are many who object to genetically modified food, even when they are told it might be the only way to feed a rapidly growing global population.\n\nClick on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', '2ef852bb-74eb-508c-a912-510e422ef7c0'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'For the purposes of argument, culture is divided into material and non-material, and the speaker''s aim is to show how they both affect each other. Material developments in tools and technology can affect non- material culture, our customs and beliefs, and the other way around. Genetics is used as an example as it has changed the way we think about life, but also our beliefs have affected its rate of development.', NULL, NULL),
  ('afe2ae89-3126-5b7e-b8b9-bec0cbc3b5fe'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'afe2ae89-3126-5b7e-b8b9-bec0cbc3b5fe'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Naming Storms', E'The practice of giving storms personal names appears to have {{1}} with Clement Wragge, an Australian meteorologist who in the 1890s entertained himself by naming storms after women, mythical {{2}} , and politicians that he didn''t like. The modern system of using personal names developed during World War II, when meteorologists began using women''s names — often those of wives or girlfriends — instead of {{3}} designations based on latitude and longitude. Short and quickly understood, names were easier to {{4}} over the radio and easier to keep straight if there was more than one storm in a given area. The system was {{5}} in 1953 when the National Weather Service put together an alphabetical list of female names to be used for storms in the Atlantic basin. Male names were added to the list in 1979 when women''s groups pointed out the sexism of using only female names.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.originated, 2.figures, 3.cumbersome, 4.transmit, 5.formalized', NULL, NULL),
  ('afe6815b-9266-54a4-802e-2a6b17ae819f'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'afe6815b-9266-54a4-802e-2a6b17ae819f'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'RS', NULL, '994e354e-95d5-514e-9395-3231a1107d2b'::uuid, NULL, E'Students who wish to apply for an extension should approach their tutors.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('afed7d3f-968d-5484-aa21-e9186dfda241'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'afed7d3f-968d-5484-aa21-e9186dfda241'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Mount Everest', E'Called Chomolungma ("goddess mother of the world") in Tibet and Sagarmatha ("goddess of the sky") in Nepal, Mount Everest once went by the pedestrian name of Peak XV among Westerners. That was before {{1}} established that it was the highest mountain on Earth, a fact that came as something of a surprise—Peak XV had seemed lost in the crowd of other formidable Himalayan peaks, many of which gave the {{2}} of greater height.\nIn 1852, the Great Trigonometrical Survey of India measured Everest''s elevation as 29,002 feet above sea level. This figure remained the officially {{4}} height for more than one hundred years. In 1955, it was adjusted by a mere 26 feet to 29,028 (8,848 m).\nThe mountain received its official name in 1865 in honor of Sir George Everest, the British Surveyor General from 1830—1843 who had mapped the Indian subcontinent. He had some {{5}} about having his name bestowed on the peak, arguing that the mountain should retain its local appellation, the standard policy of geographical societies.\nBefore the Survey of India, a number of other mountains ranked supreme in the eyes of the world. In the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries, the Andean peak Chimborazo was considered the highest. At a relatively unremarkable 20,561 feet (6,310 m), it is in fact nowhere near the highest, {{3}} by about thirty other Andean peaks and several dozen in the Himalayas. In 1809, the Himalayan peak Dhaulagiri (26,810 feet; 8,172 m) was declared the ultimate, only to be shunted aside in 1840 by Kanchenjunga (28,208 feet; 8,598 m), which today ranks third. Everest''s status has been unrivaled for the last century-and-a-half, but not without a few threats.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.surveyors, 2.illusion, 3.accepted, 4.reservations, 5.surpassed', NULL, NULL),
  ('aff28ee2-77fd-52cd-9988-0e60eff368d5'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_ESSAY', E'WRITE_ESSAY', E'WRITING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'aff28ee2-77fd-52cd-9988-0e60eff368d5'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Higher Salaries', E'Because university degrees can get people jobs with higher salaries, university students should pay full cost for their education. Do you agree or not?', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 200, 300),
