  ('bd0dcce6-a6fb-5454-8cd1-970a092e9db6'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'bd0dcce6-a6fb-5454-8cd1-970a092e9db6'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Food and US', E'Perhaps it should come as no surprise then that food has been a medium for the nation''s defining struggles, whether at the Boston Tea Party or the sit-ins at southern lunch counters. It is integral to our concepts of health and even morality whether one refrains from alcohol for religious reasons or evades meat for political {{1}} .\nBut strong opinions have not brought {{2}} . Americans are ambivalent about what they put in their mouths. We have become {{3}} of our foods, especially as we learn more about what they contain.\nThe {{4}} of food is still prosperous in the American consciousness. It''s no coincidence, then, that the first Thanksgiving holds the American imagination in such bondage. It''s what we eat — and how we {{5}} it with friends, family, and strangers — that help define America as a community today.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.ideals, 2.certainty, 3.suspicious, 4.ritual, 5.share', NULL, NULL),
