  ('c34d3081-f6a1-5412-8652-030c8730da14'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'c34d3081-f6a1-5412-8652-030c8730da14'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'ASQ', E'What is the occupational title for a person who composes novels?', '2d8def13-bafa-547c-b747-5f0029324833'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Novelist', NULL, NULL),
  ('c35ab130-ebf5-5df7-a273-face470db7b7'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'c35ab130-ebf5-5df7-a273-face470db7b7'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'World Cup', E'You may think that the World Cup, like the Olympic Games, only occurs once every four years. It is the final rounds that take {{2}} every four years, but the competition as a whole is an ongoing {{3}} , since the qualifying rounds take place over the preceding three years. The final phase of the tournament now involves thirty-two teams competing over a four-week {{4}} in a previously nominated {{5}} nation. It has become the most widely-viewed sporting event in the world.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.place, 2.event, 3.period, 4.host', NULL, NULL),
  ('c364746f-60ad-566b-8fcc-35e7764f02fb'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'SUMMARIZE_WRITTEN_TEXT', E'SUMMARIZE_WRITTEN_TEXT', E'WRITING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'c364746f-60ad-566b-8fcc-35e7764f02fb'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Crocodiles and Alligators', E'A crocodile has a ‘V’ shaped snout while an alligator has a rounded ‘U’ shaped snout. A crocodile’s upper and lower jaws are different sizes so their teeth are always exposed. An alligator has even jaws hiding all but a little of its upper teeth. Crocodiles and alligators are different colors too: crocodiles are olive-brown and alligators are dark-brown or dark green.\nAlligators and crocodiles live in different parts of the world, though obviously many are housed in foreign zoos. Alligators are mostly found in eastern China and the southern parts of the United States and crocodiles are found in Australia, Africa, south-east Asia, and across the American continent.\nBecause crocodiles have special glands to help them cope with salt, they are quite often found near salt water though they also like fresh water. Alligators are more likely to be found in fresh water lakes and rivers but they can tolerate salt to a certain extent. Crocodiles and alligators are both from the order Crocodilia but only crocodiles are ‘true’ crocodiles; the animals are taxonomically different.\nBoth of these ancient looking beasts are highly dangerous and they are fast on land and water. If you are close enough to a crocodile or alligator for him to see you smile, you are in big trouble: unless you’re up a tree or behind a zoo fence.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 5, 75),
  ('c36a2a47-6a00-5a02-b9b9-730a36d3de09'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'c36a2a47-6a00-5a02-b9b9-730a36d3de09'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'RS', NULL, 'bc282d34-1f32-5823-8a90-ced0afbdbd70'::uuid, NULL, E'Two thousand years ago, there were few people in the world.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('c36acbc9-d244-518e-a20e-aefa6f829052'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'c36acbc9-d244-518e-a20e-aefa6f829052'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'WFD', NULL, '166b3748-966b-501a-af96-c10c17b9e7a6'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'The software companies design and create new products.', NULL, NULL),
  ('c36cb5b2-e224-5359-951d-f5b5d9892d30'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'SUMMARIZE_WRITTEN_TEXT', E'SUMMARIZE_WRITTEN_TEXT', E'WRITING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'c36cb5b2-e224-5359-951d-f5b5d9892d30'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Benefit of Honey', E'In order to have a competitive edge, athletes often use drugs with high athletic performance.\nThe National Honey Board recently found that honey has the same functions but less negative impact. This clinical trial is the third in a series of studies focusing on the use of honey by athletes. The first study (involving 71 subjects) determined that honey has a milder effect on blood sugar than other popular forms of carbohydrate gel.\nThe second study in the series (with 39 weight trained subjects) investigated the combination of honey with a protein supplement and suggested that honey speeds muscle recovery after a workout.', NULL, NULL, E'Honey, with the same functions as drugs used by athletes for high athletic performance and less negative impact, has a milder effect on blood sugar than other popular forms of carbohydrate gel, and speeds muscle recovery after a workout.', NULL, 5, 75),
  ('c374846b-d547-5d62-89fe-7c64ca7ea590'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'READ_ALOUD', E'READ_ALOUD', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'c374846b-d547-5d62-89fe-7c64ca7ea590'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'MBA', E'Exhilarating, exhausting and intense. There are just some of the words used to describe doing an MBA. Everyone’s experience of doing MBA is, of course, different through denying that it’s hard and a demanding work whichever course you do. MBA is one of the fastest growing areas of studying in the UK so that must be a sustainable benefit against form in one pain.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('c37ef13d-6656-5dbb-bbd7-4f6f693562e8'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'c37ef13d-6656-5dbb-bbd7-4f6f693562e8'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'RS', NULL, 'c1e2613f-aca2-5350-84af-8a0a86331b91'::uuid, NULL, E'Students should book a library tour in the first week of the first semester.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('c38217a2-0af5-5e84-aa9c-31bef5a03858'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'c38217a2-0af5-5e84-aa9c-31bef5a03858'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Ecological Footprint', NULL, '8ad278d0-f150-5685-a262-62af801b0de8'::uuid, NULL, E'A maker has standards to control qualities of his or her products.\nAccording to the data collected by WWF, over last three decades about one third of natural resource has been damaged.\nHuman have consumed energy resources more than the sustainable amount by twenty percent.\nEvery country should record its ecological footprint. The ecological footprint is to calculate how much land each resident needs for survival.\nThe land available for an individual''s survival has decreased from two point one hectares to one point eight hectares in the globe.\nAnd this means a total of eleven point eight billion hectares is required globally.\nThe ecological footprint for Australia is seven point seven.\nAnd if the global average rises to something on the Australian consumption level, by 2050, we will need at least two extra planets to support our consumption.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('c3898904-d048-580a-8257-ba41a706c8cc'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'c3898904-d048-580a-8257-ba41a706c8cc'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'RS', NULL, 'd027cacb-3b19-5b8f-93a5-e45e02e08243'::uuid, NULL, E'Politicians and marketing professionals have a good understanding of persuasion.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('c391d423-44f6-50a0-8d2c-f23b5e1b1656'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'c391d423-44f6-50a0-8d2c-f23b5e1b1656'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Public Realm', NULL, 'd94a2afa-23e6-5994-acfc-5f310d025994'::uuid, NULL, E'The public realm in America has two roles: it is the dwelling place of our civilization and our civic life, and it is the physical manifestation of the common good. And when you degrade the public realm, you will automatically degrade the quality of your civic life and the character of all the enactments of your public life and communal life that take place there. The public realm comes mostly in the form of the street in America because we don’t have the 1,000-year-old cathedral plazas and market squares of older cultures. And your ability to define space and to create places that are worth caring about all comes from a body of culture that we call the culture of civic design. This is a body of knowledge, method, skill and principle that we threw in the garbage after World War II and decided we don’t need that anymore; we’re not going to use it. And consequently, we can see the result all around us. The public realm has to inform us not only where we are geographically, but it has to inform us where we are in our culture. Where we’ve come from, what kind of people we are, and it needs to, by doing that, it needs to afford us a glimpse to where we’re going in order to allow us to dwell in a hopeful present.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('c395108c-4788-568a-9e37-a24e1a5b0399'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'c395108c-4788-568a-9e37-a24e1a5b0399'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Conservation', E'Species {{1}} is the most obvious aspect of the loss of biodiversity. For example, species form the bulk of the examples in a comprehensive assessment of the state of the planet published in the early 21st century by the Millennium Ecosystem Assessment, an international effort {{2}} by the United Nations Environment Programme. The subject of conservation is broader than this, however. Even a species that survives extinction can lose much of its genetic diversity as local, genetically distinct populations are lost from most of the species’ original range. Furthermore, ecosystems may {{3}} dramatically in area and lose many of their functions, even if their constituent species manage to survive. Conservation is involved with studying all these kinds of losses, understanding the factors responsible for them, developing techniques to {{4}} losses, and, whenever possible, restoring biodiversity.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.extinction, 2.coordinated, 3.shrink, 4.prevent', NULL, NULL),
  ('c39a6dc5-bbae-59c5-9645-d2b99ffc572e'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'SUMMARIZE_WRITTEN_TEXT', E'SUMMARIZE_WRITTEN_TEXT', E'WRITING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'c39a6dc5-bbae-59c5-9645-d2b99ffc572e'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Parosmia', E'Parosmia is a disorder characterized by a change in the perception of odors. Most often, the smells are unpleasant, like hand soap smelling like dead bodies and the outdoors like sewage. This odd distortion of scent is being seen in a growing number of people who have contracted COVID-19, lost their sense of smell and taste, and recovered from the virus but never fully regained their sense of smell again, says Dr. Jennifer Grayson, director of otolaryngology research at the University of Alabama at Birmingham. "Parosmia is not classified as a long-haul symptom of COVID. It''s more of a complicating and potentially permanent factor of COVID," she says. Parosmia is closely related to phantosmia, an olfactory hallucination characterized by smelling something that isn''t there, such as smelling smoke when there''s nothing burning, Grayson says. It can also be intertwined with a distorted sense of taste known as dysgeusia. For example, a member of a Parosmia/Post-COVID Facebook group described toothpaste tasting like "how a landfill smells." These odd odors and tastes are described by other Facebook group members as "dumpster juice," "hot garbage," "potting soil," "rotting flesh" and "dog." They can greatly diminish quality of life. Eating becomes excruciating difficult, as most things that tasted pleasant before they developed parosmia suddenly cause sufferers to gag or vomit. Because parosmia can linger for months, many of these individuals lose weight and some become depressed and hopeless for fear they will never regain their sense of smell and taste.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 5, 75),
  ('c3a2c612-e180-57c2-837d-21be9f7a081a'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'c3a2c612-e180-57c2-837d-21be9f7a081a'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'ASQ', E'What do we call the property someone leaves to others in a will?', '8d03bd18-57b8-5e59-b834-16755b39cabf'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Inheritance / legacy', NULL, NULL),
  ('c3a32ce0-8623-5cf9-8f08-aaa5155d699f'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'c3a32ce0-8623-5cf9-8f08-aaa5155d699f'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'RS', NULL, 'd7e6b39f-a0bb-5f84-b25c-3738fa77a5d7'::uuid, NULL, E'More patients were infected in this outbreak than in any prior ones.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('c3a512c6-8751-5a6d-8d10-89a68fb31cbf'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RE_ORDER_PARAGRAPHS', E'RE_ORDER_PARAGRAPHS', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'c3a512c6-8751-5a6d-8d10-89a68fb31cbf'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'One Week Before(一周前)', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, E'2, 3, 4, 1', NULL, NULL),
  ('c3a5d7cf-961a-598e-863d-38f70b4d05a2'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'c3a5d7cf-961a-598e-863d-38f70b4d05a2'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'WFD', NULL, 'aeff3b07-cbb9-5b5f-8fac-735bb79f06c4'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Each new product would have a relatively long life cycle.', NULL, NULL),
  ('c3ad99c3-894d-5910-89ec-e1091e0ad793'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'MC_LISTENING_MULTIPLE', E'MC_LISTENING_MULTIPLE', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'c3ad99c3-894d-5910-89ec-e1091e0ad793'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Community Tension', E'In places where there''ve been serious community tensions — I mean, Brickendon''s a good example of this — there were 250 reported incidents there in 2009, often to do with neighbors and arguments, but criminal activity too. Um, and the police will tell you that if you leave residents to sort things out for themselves, the number of incidents rises — it doesn''t fall.\nSo the local council realized that they had to do something. So they went round to people''s houses and met them and their neighbors, and talked to everyone and got a general idea of what the local concerns were.\nIf there were extended families involved, they looked at those particular families more closely and all the relatives, and got some of the family intervention services to talk to them. And there were some very positive results.\nI mean, you can pick out the hotspots — you know, the main areas for crime — but the hotspots don''t tell you what causes the crime in the first place, and so don''t address the issues.\nSo this proactive, more direct approach by the council worked well. And this was followed up with other quite practical solutions, like cleaning up the area, getting rid of debris in alleyways, and just making the place somewhere more agreeable — more liveable.\n\nAccording to the speaker, which of the following measures helped reduce community problems in Brickendon?', '42edfedc-268d-521a-b110-50645396628a'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'interviewing residents about the problems\nremoving litter from the streets', NULL, NULL),
  ('c3aef458-d05f-5a1d-b672-e2966570616c'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'c3aef458-d05f-5a1d-b672-e2966570616c'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'ASQ', E'How many states are there in America?', 'daac5c42-96b2-5e01-8cba-990d8ea8e7bd'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Fifty', NULL, NULL),
  ('c3b29b74-993e-5898-99aa-17df512fb633'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'c3b29b74-993e-5898-99aa-17df512fb633'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'RS', NULL, '45eacd83-66f8-582c-91d5-7ca2e54d9c02'::uuid, NULL, E'Journalists need to work with a range of technologies.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('c3bfa285-1461-5df6-88d8-3a2ac551c0f3'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'c3bfa285-1461-5df6-88d8-3a2ac551c0f3'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'WFD', NULL, '9bda64f8-31f6-5276-bdd5-266045ad0e6b'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'The graph shows that there hasn''t been a lot of growth recently.', NULL, NULL),
  ('c3c035e8-691f-55ea-8beb-8f75c5c92c0b'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'SELECT_MISSING_WORD', E'SELECT_MISSING_WORD', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'c3c035e8-691f-55ea-8beb-8f75c5c92c0b'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Cognitive Therapy', E'The first study that used mindfulness-based cognitive therapy with depression it was pretty spectacular. It was done at Oxford University and it was done with people whose depression is so severe that nothing helps, no medication helps, electric shock doesn’t help, medicine psychiatry doesn’t know what to do. People get depressed very deeply, they recover and then they get depressed again. So with that group they use mindfulness-based cognitive therapy and they found that it cut the rate of relapse (of having depression again) by 50 percent. If this were a drug, some pharmaceutical company would be making billions of dollars, but it’s not a drug. It’s free basically. So mindfulness-based cognitive therapy works very well for...\n\nAt the end of the recording the lost word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', 'c2287bcc-61a6-5def-8d5a-4d9f95317e45'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'depression', NULL, NULL),
  ('c3c162d8-33ab-529a-9525-6638ee4c5e10'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'WRITE_FROM_DICTATION', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'c3c162d8-33ab-529a-9525-6638ee4c5e10'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'WFD', NULL, 'dda2b14c-7260-5c34-b205-6f26bebebf37'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'The weekly lectures will be presented by the coordinating professor.', NULL, NULL),
  ('c3c41aaa-880c-567e-9781-b6ae18ae6cbf'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RE_ORDER_PARAGRAPHS', E'RE_ORDER_PARAGRAPHS', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'c3c41aaa-880c-567e-9781-b6ae18ae6cbf'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Mars', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, E'4, 1, 2, 3', NULL, NULL),
  ('c3c53f96-8a77-55db-8fd2-e8a8275cab04'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'c3c53f96-8a77-55db-8fd2-e8a8275cab04'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Plains Indians', E'"The Plains Indians were people who did not like to live in one place.\nThey liked to travel around and moved camps {{1}} three times a year.\nFor this reason they lived in tepees. These were {{2}} big tents and were easy to put up and take down.\nThese tepees were transported by horses."\n"Inside the tepee you would find all the items that people needed to live with.\nThe Plains Indians would decorate the insides with pictures, and store their weapons and food. The Indians would also have a fire {{3}} of the tepee to cook the food. The Sioux people {{4}} put buffalo skins on the floor to use as carpets. You would also find their beds."\n"In the Indian camp everyone had a job to do.\nThe men had to hunt for food, and keep the families safe.\nThe women had to cook all the meals, make the clothes, look {{5}} the children and whenever the camp moved they had to take down and put up the tepees."', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.at least, 2.like, 3.in the middle, 4.used to, 5.after', NULL, NULL),
  ('c3c5ad42-27d5-52a0-bb24-85f8d2e20ca2'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'MC_LISTENING_SINGLE', E'MC_LISTENING_SINGLE', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'c3c5ad42-27d5-52a0-bb24-85f8d2e20ca2'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'MCS-L', E'So, I want to discuss dissertations now. Our experience is that final year undergraduates and Masters students can struggle to find a suitable topic for their dissertation. Often they''re not able to come up with a viable or suitable area to research. Another problem may be that the topiC they choose is too broad and needs to be refined.\nSo. anyway, eventually their proposal is submitted and approved but then. after some background reading or a deeper consideration of research methods they may well want to modify their original proposal. to some extent. Well, that''s fine. We expect modifications as the work progresses. This is normal. What we don''t want to happen is that the student repeatedly wants to completely change the whole approved topic or original proposal to something completely new, and that does happen at times.\n\nWhat does the speaker say about dissertations?', '24919cfb-1b3f-560c-a203-7d0e6e4d546f'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'The subject area selected may not have sufficient focus.', NULL, NULL),
  ('c3c9e2ed-2bd5-5a7a-b935-c4124f9cefe1'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RESPOND_TO_A_SITUATION', E'RESPOND_TO_A_SITUATION', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'c3c9e2ed-2bd5-5a7a-b935-c4124f9cefe1'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Asking for a Ride', E'You haven’t taken a taxi, and you want to ask your colleague for a ride. What should you say to your colleague?', 'eefc6291-9df3-559c-b488-d3d8035a72d4'::uuid, NULL, E'Hey colleague, I want to talk to you about something.\nWe seem to have a little issue with my way home.\nIt''s like I didn’t take a taxi, so I wonder if I can get a ride with you.\nAnd I''d like to know what you think.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('c3d2f64f-1110-5da0-a75a-560557eabc49'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'c3d2f64f-1110-5da0-a75a-560557eabc49'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'ASQ', E'What do we call animals that eat only plants?', '6cb83cdd-9f03-56c4-aaed-9ad9054dff2e'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Herbivores', NULL, NULL),
  ('c3d4eb88-053d-5b12-8eae-19f2af36756c'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'MC_LISTENING_SINGLE', E'MC_LISTENING_SINGLE', E'LISTENING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'c3d4eb88-053d-5b12-8eae-19f2af36756c'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Financial Intimacy', E'MIGAKI: In your role as a financial therapist, you''ve got sort of a five-step process for something that you call financial intimacy. I''m wondering if you can help us define that and talk us through it.\nCLAYMAN: So with the first one, equality, what we want to capture here in a healthy financial dynamic is that both partners have equal say and equal power about what''s going to happen with the money. So sometimes when a couple comes to me with an inequality imbalance, there''s a partner that either has - maybe is bringing in the bulk of the income and then thus feels like they should have more say. Or sometimes there''s a partner that''s just more anxious about money and so really needs money to be a source of control for them and feels like their partner is just - in not agreeing with them and not doing what they say should be done with the money, that this is threatening to them. And they want their partner to kind of come to heel, if you will. So where I would approach that is, all right, well, we need to have both parties present in the decisions about the money. Just because you''re more anxious or because you make more of a contribution to the income doesn''t mean that you get an outsized say in this.\n\nAccording to the financial therapist, why does a couple experience the inequality imbalance?', '1b7881ff-ef90-5814-8aaf-b6369547513f'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Because one contributes more to the income and believes this gives more say.', NULL, NULL),
  ('c3d8cd44-180c-547b-af1d-5359e3505e28'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'c3d8cd44-180c-547b-af1d-5359e3505e28'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'ASQ', E'What gas is breathed out by human beings from the lungs?', '059d0742-e5df-57b7-8fa7-4e52214ec182'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Carbon dioxide', NULL, NULL),
