  ('c7c5b808-6270-5727-b203-8de39390b856'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'c7c5b808-6270-5727-b203-8de39390b856'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Computer', E'The purpose of this paper is to consider the claim, often made, that computer simulation exercises provide an excellent source of speaking practice. In so doing I shall first consider the properties of computer simulations from a theoretical {{1}} , then describe the experience of {{2}} a particular simulation with a general EFL class. On the basis of this experience, and of some very straightforward pedagogical considerations, I shall argue that the claim is justified, {{3}} to a very important caveat: computer simulations can form the basis of excellent speaking exercises, provided you do not expect the computer to do all the work. Put in another way, many computer simulations only {{4}} their full potential as language exercises if they are {{5}} into a larger, planned, teacher-managed activity.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.point of view, 2.using, 3.subject, 4.attain, 5.integrated', NULL, NULL),
