  ('cb9e0c18-c456-5b5a-920d-8bb4ccee2f5a'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'READ_ALOUD', E'READ_ALOUD', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'cb9e0c18-c456-5b5a-920d-8bb4ccee2f5a'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Business Communication', E'Much of today''s business is conducted across international borders, and while the majority of the global business community might share the use of English as a common language, the nuances and expectations of business communication might differ greatly from culture to culture.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('cba726b9-f405-5e4e-a8ba-2dd9984b3e55'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'WRITE_ESSAY', E'WRITE_ESSAY', E'WRITING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'cba726b9-f405-5e4e-a8ba-2dd9984b3e55'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Good Boss', E'Having a good boss in the workplace is very important for personal development. What kind of boss is a good boss for you?', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 200, 300),
  ('cba87d31-4076-5850-8ff5-cab914cfdef1'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'cba87d31-4076-5850-8ff5-cab914cfdef1'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'ASQ', E'What will you hear after a flash of lightning?', '896fd315-4e9b-5ea8-afba-374813973f57'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Thunder', NULL, NULL),
  ('cbb2d7d2-8c67-5310-880c-3e4687cc51be'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RE_ORDER_PARAGRAPHS', E'RE_ORDER_PARAGRAPHS', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'cbb2d7d2-8c67-5310-880c-3e4687cc51be'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'American Housing', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, E'4, 5, 6, 1, 2, 3', NULL, NULL),
  ('cbb3d58f-a733-5afb-8609-544c1867e9cb'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'cbb3d58f-a733-5afb-8609-544c1867e9cb'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Creativity', NULL, '4de895d7-8726-5fbf-90f5-f13ced0521f5'::uuid, NULL, E'Here are three important factors in creativity: people, process and product. The most important one is the process.\nFirst you have to create the right person through education with a creative mind. Second, you have to create the right process to have people engaged in innovation process. Third, you need to find the right problem to work on. Human beings can survive and prosper largely depending on the creativity they have. If you identify and assess the creativity of a finished product, it is taken as a proxy for the creativity of the person who produced such a product. Therefore, a creative product should be surprising, original, beautiful and useful. People should have factors necessary for genius, ability, and right mindset. You should improve to imitate and change insight look from new perspectives, innovatively create something with imagination to expand conceptual spaces.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('cbb78275-c988-5aae-8d72-ec0dd2b89a56'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'MC_READING_MULTIPLE', E'MC_READING_MULTIPLE', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'cbb78275-c988-5aae-8d72-ec0dd2b89a56'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'MCM-R', E'Sensitivity to physical laws is thus an important consideration for the maker of applied-art objects. It is often taken for granted that this is also true for the maker of fine-art objects. This assumption misses a significant difference between the two disciplines. Fine-art objects are not constrained by the laws of physics in the same way that applied-art objects are. Because their primary purpose is not functional, they are only limited in terms of the materials used to make them. Sculptures must, for example, be stable, which requires an understanding of the properties of mass, weight distribution, and stress. Paintings must have rigid stretchers so that the canvas will be taut, and the paint must not deteriorate, crack, or discolor. These are problems that must be overcome by the artist because they tend to intrude upon his or her conception of the work. For example, in the early Italian Renaissance, bronze statues of horses with a raised foreleg usually had a cannonball under that hoof. This was done because the cannonball was needed to support the weight of the leg. In other words, the demands of the laws of physics, not the sculptor''s aesthetic intentions, placed the ball there. That this device was a necessary structural compromise is clear from the fact that the cannonball quickly disappeared when sculptors learned how to strengthen the internal structure of a statue with iron braces (iron being much stronger than bronze).\n\nAccording to paragraph, sculptors in the Italian Renaissance stopped using cannonballs in bronze statues of horses because', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'they began using a material that much stronger\nthey found a way to strengthen the statues internally', NULL, NULL),
  ('cbbd1690-91e5-5d09-9118-f216d20d3f04'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'cbbd1690-91e5-5d09-9118-f216d20d3f04'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'RS', NULL, 'd2747514-1512-55b7-ae66-ffb67dc14675'::uuid, NULL, E'When writing this report, I have omitted all unnecessary details.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('cbbeb9a1-d0de-5651-96d0-79f1a4bc1a8f'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'REPEAT_SENTENCE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'cbbeb9a1-d0de-5651-96d0-79f1a4bc1a8f'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'RS', NULL, '10890eb1-6ca6-5c49-b91e-b9c773438e4c'::uuid, NULL, E'She ate her sandwich alongside several other office workers taking their break.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('cbc40004-a551-5d5c-8faa-1ed273f776c0'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DROPDOWN', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'cbc40004-a551-5d5c-8faa-1ed273f776c0'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Color for Propaganda', E'Most of our reaction to color is subconscious and “consumers are generally {{1}} of the pervasive and persuasive effects of color,” according to J. H. Kleynhans, author of The Use of Color as a Tool for Propaganda. Kleynhans explains how the psychological effects of color can {{2}} senses with the power of suggestion: “The power that color wields is seen at every level of communication: in corporate identification and logos, signage, advertising, on television, billboards, in print media and packaging, on the Internet and at point of purchase.”\nWe can see the effects and power of color reflected in {{3}} behavior. For example, according to color theorist Leatrice Eiseman, when we are rushing down the aisles in a supermarket, we only spend around .03 seconds looking at a package. In that moment, we make quick decisions {{4}} on colors that inform a package and its contents. Those same colors inform whether the product is something appealing to our eyes, taste, psyches, and pockets.', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.unaware, 2.stimulate, 3.consumer, 4.based', NULL, NULL),
