  ('f2fc0939-7e5d-58c3-9fb8-b68887df475a'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'ANSWER_SHORT_QUESTION', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'f2fc0939-7e5d-58c3-9fb8-b68887df475a'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'ASQ', E'What do we call a baby dog?', '11a8725a-4777-5595-b6d3-c697fdbbba76'::uuid, NULL, NULL, E'Puppy', NULL, NULL),
  ('f2fdca4c-5f9a-5131-8020-712acefdcd83'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'READ_ALOUD', E'READ_ALOUD', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'f2fdca4c-5f9a-5131-8020-712acefdcd83'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Productive Capacity', E'The core of the problem was the immense disparity between the country’s productive capacity and the ability of people to consume. Great innovations in productive techniques during and after the war raised the output of industry beyond the purchasing capacity of U.S. farmers and wage earners.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('f3099c4a-7c70-5fa1-b4f0-295e2739d40e'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RE_ORDER_PARAGRAPHS', E'RE_ORDER_PARAGRAPHS', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'f3099c4a-7c70-5fa1-b4f0-295e2739d40e'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Palm Oil', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, E'4, 3, 1, 2', NULL, NULL),
  ('f3161768-e1ac-5e54-842c-6a40fd2f65e7'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'f3161768-e1ac-5e54-842c-6a40fd2f65e7'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Stress', E'Stress is what you feel when you have to handle more than you are used to. When you are stressed, your body {{1}} as though you are in danger. It makes {{2}} that speed up your heart, make you breathe faster, and give you a burst of energy. This is called the fight-or-flight stress {{3}} . Some stress is normal and even useful. Stress can help if you need to work hard or react quickly. For example, it can help you win a race or {{4}} an important job on time. But if stress happens too often or lasts too long, it can have bad effects. It can be linked to headaches, an upset stomach, back pain, and trouble sleeping. It can weaken your {{5}} system, making it harder to fight off {{6}} .', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.responds, 2.hormones, 3.response, 4.finish, 5.immune, 6.disease', NULL, NULL),
  ('f31cfe09-637d-5111-984d-e5b3b6a6d2f0'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'SUMMARIZE_WRITTEN_TEXT', E'SUMMARIZE_WRITTEN_TEXT', E'WRITING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'f31cfe09-637d-5111-984d-e5b3b6a6d2f0'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Bullwhip Effect', E'The term bullwhip effect was coined in 1961 by MIT computer scientist Jay Forrester in his seminal book "Industrial Dynamics." It describes what happens when fluctuations in demand reverberate and amplify throughout the supply chain, leading to worsening problems and shortages. Imagine the physics of cracking a whip. It starts with a small flick of the wrist, but the whip''s wave patterns grow exponentially in a chain reaction, leading to the tip, a snap — and a sharp pain for anyone on the receiving end.\nThe same thing can happen in supply chains when orders for a product from a retailer go up or down by some amount and that gets amplified by wholesalers, distributors and raw material suppliers.\nThe onset of the COVID-19 pandemic, which led to lengthy lockdowns, massive unemployment and a whole host of other effects that messed up global supply chains, essentially supercharged the bullwhip''s snap.\nThe supply of autos is one such example. New as well as used vehicles have been in short supply throughout the pandemic, at times forcing consumers to wait as long as a year for the most popular models. In early 2020, when the pandemic put most Americans in lockdown, carmakers began to anticipate a fall in demand, so they significantly scaled back production. This sent a signal to suppliers, especially of computer chips, that they would need to find different buyers for their products.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 5, 75),
  ('f3284fbe-06d6-585b-b063-38b721a7d01c'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'READ_ALOUD', E'READ_ALOUD', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'f3284fbe-06d6-585b-b063-38b721a7d01c'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Adulthood', E'Once most animals reach adulthood, they stop growing. In contrast, even plants that are thousands of years old continue to grow new needles, add new wood, and produce cones and new flowers, almost as if parts of their bodies remained "forever young". The secrets of plant growth are regions of tissue that can produce cells that later develop into specialized tissues.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('f32ab6db-38a6-5602-b112-81de4a05925e'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'RE_TELL_LECTURE', E'SPEAKING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'f32ab6db-38a6-5602-b112-81de4a05925e'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Winter Blues', NULL, '439019fc-8df3-5ec9-84c2-7ca0e59be1b6'::uuid, NULL, E'A lot of people can’t get used to the short days or longer nights during the winter months. There are a lot of theories as to why that occurs. One of the theories is that we become de-synchronised with the light-dark patterns because we don’t get enough light during the day. So, some people can’t really adapt to that. So they start having feelings like depression, withdrawal, carbohydrate craving, weight gain. And that typically goes away during the summer months. So that’s really what characterises what we call Seasonal Affective Disorder known as ‘winter blues’. I think there are huge individual differences, but we know of studies where it appears that that maybe 20 minutes of daily dosage of strong light is enough. And then we’re talking about a light that resemble a daylight or an outdoor exposure. And this light dosage should be presented during morning hours. There’s a couple of things that can be done. First of all, obviously go outdoor during the day. If you can, spend one to two hours outside. If you can’t do that, sit by a window. But don’t sit against the window. Sit facing the window because that’s where you want to get the light. You want the light at the eye. And the third option is (to) increase the amount of light that you have around you. So if you’re going to sit at a desk and work from home, add table lamps add lamps around your computer screen. Add something that would increase that amount of light at the eye.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
  ('f32ba865-f7ec-5242-9b57-3345cf6a3b0d'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'FILL_IN_THE_BLANKS_DRAG_AND_DROP', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'f32ba865-f7ec-5242-9b57-3345cf6a3b0d'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Computational Thinking', E'Developing computational thinking helps students to better understand the world around them. Many of us happily drive a car without understanding what goes on under the {{1}} . So is it necessary for children to learn how to {{2}} computers? After all, some experts say coding is one of the human skills that will become obsolete as artificial intelligence grows. Nevertheless, governments believe coding is an essential skill. Since 2014, the principles of computer programming have featured on England’s {{3}} for children from the age of five or six, when they start primary school. While not all children will become programmers, Mark Martin, a computing teacher at Sydenham High School, London, argues that they should learn to understand what makes computers work and try to solve problems as a computer {{4}} .', NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1.bonnet, 2.program, 3.curriculum, 4.might', NULL, NULL),
  ('f32db380-78b8-57bd-83ff-5fbf97690f06'::uuid, TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-23 00:00:00+00', FALSE, E'RE_ORDER_PARAGRAPHS', E'RE_ORDER_PARAGRAPHS', E'READING', E'SHARED', NULL, E'APPROVED', 'f32db380-78b8-57bd-83ff-5fbf97690f06'::uuid, 1, NULL, TRUE, 0, NULL, E'Scientist Kepler(科学家开普勒)', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, E'1, 2, 3, 4', NULL, NULL),
